window.REVISION_SUBJECTS.push({"config":{"brandIcon":"✡️","brandSubtitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062)","brandTitle":"Judaism (AQA RS A)","breadcrumb":"Religious Studies / AQA A / Judaism","examBoard":"AQA GCSE","footerNote":"Original practice questions written to the AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification, Component 1: Judaism. They are not past-paper material. Model answers are indicative exemplars, not official AQA mark schemes, and other valid answers are credited.","heroEyebrow":"Component 1 · Judaism · Beliefs \u0026 teachings · Practices","markingSchemaId":"aqa-rs-generic","pageTitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A – Judaism Revision","papers":[{"id":"Beliefs","label":"Beliefs \u0026 teachings","subtitle":"Key beliefs · The Covenant and the mitzvot (1.1–1.10)","title":"Judaism: beliefs and teachings"},{"id":"Practices","label":"Practices","subtitle":"The synagogue and worship · Family life and festivals (2.1–2.13)","title":"Judaism: practices"}],"storageId":"aqa_rs_a_judaism","subjectArea":"Religious Studies","subjectName":"Judaism"},"questions":{"paper1":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"Which one of the following is the Jewish declaration of faith that God is one?\nA Amidah\nB Shema\nC Kaddish\nD Havdalah","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – the Shema. Deuteronomy 6:4, \"Hear, O Israel: the Lord is our God, the Lord is One\", declares God's oneness. The Amidah is the standing prayer, the Kaddish praises God (and is said by mourners), and Havdalah ends Shabbat.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 6:4 (the Shema)"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"God as one"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"Which one of the following best describes God as Creator in Judaism?\nA God made the world from materials that already existed alongside God\nB God created the universe and everything in it, and it was good\nC God created only the people of Israel\nD God created the world and then took no further interest in it","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B. Genesis 1:1 and 1:31 teach that God created everything and saw that it was \"very good\". Judaism teaches that God created freely and remains involved with creation, so D is wrong.","evidence":["Genesis 1:1","Genesis 1:31"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"God as Creator"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"Give two ways in which Jews show their belief that God is one.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Reciting the Shema morning and evening.\n• Fixing a mezuzah containing the Shema to their doorposts.\n• Wearing tefillin, which contain the Shema, at weekday morning prayer.\n• Refusing to make or worship images of God.\n• Saying the Shema as their last words before death.\n• Singing Yigdal or affirming Maimonides' principle of God's unity.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 6:4–9","Exodus 20:3–5"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"God as one"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"Give two qualities of God that Jews believe are shown in God being both Judge and merciful.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Justice: God judges actions fairly.\n• Mercy: God is compassionate and forgiving.\n• Love: God cares for creation and for the covenant people.\n• Patience: God is \"slow to anger\".\n• Faithfulness: God keeps promises.\n• Willingness to forgive those who repent (teshuvah).","evidence":["Exodus 34:6–7"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"God as Judge, loving and merciful"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in God as Creator influences Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Treating every person with dignity. Jews believe God created humans \"in the image of God\" (Genesis 1:27), so every person has equal worth. This shapes how they treat others, for example by giving tzedakah and opposing prejudice, because to harm a person is to disrespect God's image.\n\n2. Keeping Shabbat and caring for creation. God rested on the seventh day after creating the world, so Jews stop creative work on Shabbat to remember that the world belongs to God. Because the world is God's creation, many Jews also see caring for the environment as a religious duty, linked to the command not to destroy needlessly (bal tashchit). The festival of Tu Bishvat, the new year for trees, is often used today for tree planting.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Genesis 2:2–3","bal tashchit (Deuteronomy 20:19)"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"God as Creator"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"Explain two contrasting Jewish understandings of God as Law-Giver.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Orthodox Jews believe God gave the whole Torah, both the written law and the oral law later recorded in the Talmud, to Moses at Sinai. The 613 mitzvot are therefore God's unchanging commands, and halakhah (Jewish law) must be obeyed in full, for example the Shabbat and kosher laws, because the commands come straight from God.\n\n2. Reform Jews believe the Torah was inspired by God but written by human beings over time, so God's will is understood through a developing, \"progressive\" revelation. Ethical commands, such as justice and loving one's neighbour, remain binding, but individuals may make an informed choice about ritual laws. For example, some Reform Jews do not keep strict kashrut or may drive to synagogue on Shabbat.","evidence":["Exodus 20:1–17","Torah min ha-shamayim (Torah from heaven) – Orthodox principle"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"God as Law-Giver"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"Explain two Jewish beliefs about the oneness of God. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. God is unique and indivisible. Judaism teaches that there is only one God, who cannot be split into parts or persons. The Shema states, \"Hear, O Israel: the Lord is our God, the Lord is One\" (Deuteronomy 6:4). This is why Jews recite it twice daily and fix it to their doorposts in the mezuzah.\n\n2. Only God may be worshipped, and God must not be pictured. Because God is one and has no physical form, the commandments forbid other gods and graven images (Exodus 20:3–4). Maimonides stated that God has no body. Synagogues therefore contain no statues or pictures of God, and Jews have historically refused to worship idols even under persecution.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 6:4","Exodus 20:3–4","Maimonides, Thirteen Principles of Faith (principles 2 and 3)"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"God as one"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about God as a loving and merciful Judge. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. God judges human actions. Jews believe God sees and judges everything people do. On Rosh Hashanah, tradition teaches that each person's deeds are reviewed and their fate is written in the Book of Life and sealed on Yom Kippur. This encourages Jews to take responsibility for their behaviour and to do teshuvah.\n\n2. God's judgement is always tempered by mercy. God revealed himself to Moses as \"compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in kindness\" (Exodus 34:6). The rabbis taught that God combines justice (din) and mercy (rachamim) when creating and judging the world, because strict justice alone would destroy it. So Jews trust that God will forgive those who sincerely repent, and they recite the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy during the High Holy Days.","evidence":["Exodus 34:6–7","Rabbinic teaching on din and rachamim (Genesis Rabbah 12:15)","Unetaneh Tokef prayer"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"God as Judge, loving and merciful"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"\"The most important belief about God in Judaism is that God is one.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nGod's oneness is the foundation of Judaism. The Shema, \"Hear, O Israel: the Lord is our God, the Lord is One\" (Deuteronomy 6:4), is the closest thing Judaism has to a creed. It is recited every morning and evening, placed in the mezuzah and tefillin, and traditionally said at the moment of death. A belief that is built into the daily routine and into the last words of a Jewish life is clearly central.\n\nEvery other belief about God depends on God being one. Judaism holds that the Creator, the Law-Giver and the Judge are the same single God. If there were more than one god, the Torah could not claim absolute authority and creation would have no single purpose. Maimonides placed God's unity among the first of his Thirteen Principles, and the first two commandments forbid other gods and idols. Jews have historically died rather than deny this belief, which shows its importance.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nMany Jews would say that God as Law-Giver matters more for daily life. Judaism is often described as a religion of deeds rather than creeds: what counts is keeping the mitzvot, such as Shabbat, kashrut and tzedakah. The covenant at Sinai, where God gave the Torah, is what turned Israel into a people, so belief in God as Law-Giver shapes how Jews actually live.\n\nOthers would point to God as loving and merciful. Without God's mercy, the High Holy Days and teshuvah would make no sense, and people would have no hope of forgiveness. The Thirteen Attributes (Exodus 34:6) show God choosing to be known through compassion. A Reform Jew might argue that what matters most is the ethical character of God, which calls people to justice and kindness (tikkun olam), rather than an abstract statement about God's unity.\n\nSome would also argue that it is wrong to rank the beliefs at all. The Creator, Law-Giver and Judge are aspects of one God, not competing beliefs.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nOn balance, the statement is convincing, because God's oneness is logically prior to the other beliefs: only if God is one can the Creator also be the source of the law and the merciful Judge. The strongest counter-argument is that Judaism stresses practice, but even that practice is framed by the Shema, which Jews recite as an act of worship. So oneness is the foundation, while the other beliefs explain how the one God relates to the world.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 6:4","Exodus 20:2–5","Exodus 34:6","Maimonides, Thirteen Principles"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"God as one"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","question":"\"Belief that God is a Judge is more important for Jews than belief that God is merciful.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nBelief in God as Judge gives moral seriousness to Jewish life. If God judges every action, then keeping the mitzvot matters, and wrongdoing has consequences. On Rosh Hashanah, called Yom ha-Din (the Day of Judgement), Jews believe their deeds are reviewed, and the Unetaneh Tokef prayer describes God deciding \"who shall live and who shall die\". This belief motivates Jews to examine their lives and change their behaviour.\n\nJustice is also central to the character of God in the Tenakh. Abraham asks, \"Shall not the Judge of all the earth do justly?\" (Genesis 18:25), and Deuteronomy 16:20 commands, \"Justice, justice you shall pursue.\" Jews who are judged by a just God are expected to act justly themselves, so this belief underpins the Jewish commitment to fair courts, honest business and social justice.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nMany Jews would say that judgement without mercy would be unbearable. The rabbis taught that God created the world using both strict justice and mercy, because a world judged by justice alone could not survive. God described himself to Moses as \"compassionate and gracious, slow to anger\" (Exodus 34:6), and these attributes are recited throughout the High Holy Days. The whole purpose of the Ten Days of Repentance is the hope that God's mercy will outweigh a harsh verdict.\n\nMercy also gives the belief in judgement its meaning. Jews believe that teshuvah, tefillah and tzedakah \"avert the severity of the decree\". Because God is loving, a person who sincerely returns to God can be forgiven, as the story of Jonah, read on Yom Kippur, shows with the people of Nineveh. Many Reform Jews emphasise God's love and the hope of forgiveness rather than fear of punishment.\n\nIt can also be argued that the two cannot be separated. The same God who judges is merciful, and Jews address God in prayer as Avinu Malkeinu, \"Our Father, our King\": loving like a parent and judging like a king.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is too one-sided. Judgement makes moral choices meaningful, but Jewish teaching consistently places mercy beside it, and the High Holy Day liturgy makes sense only if both are true. The stronger view is that the two beliefs are equally important and depend on each other: judgement gives Jews a reason to repent, and mercy gives them hope that repentance will be accepted.","evidence":["Genesis 18:25","Deuteronomy 16:20","Exodus 34:6","Avinu Malkeinu","Unetaneh Tokef"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"God as Judge, loving and merciful"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"What is the meaning of the Hebrew term Shekhinah?\nA The written law\nB The divine presence of God\nC The coming of the Messiah\nD The world to come","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – the divine presence. Shekhinah comes from a Hebrew root meaning \"to dwell\" and refers to God's presence among people. The written law is the Torah or Tenakh, the Messiah is the Mashiach, and the world to come is Olam Ha-Ba.","evidence":["Exodus 25:8 (v'shakhanti – \"that I may dwell\")"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"Which one of the following places was believed to be the special dwelling place of God's presence in the Temple?\nA The bimah\nB The women's gallery\nC The Holy of Holies\nD The outer courtyard","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C – the Holy of Holies, the innermost room of the Temple. Only the High Priest entered it, once a year on Yom Kippur. The bimah is a synagogue reading platform.","evidence":["1 Kings 8:10–11","Leviticus 16 (High Priest on the Day of Atonement)"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah in the Temple"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"Give two ways in which the Shekhinah is described in the Tenakh.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• As a cloud that filled the Tabernacle (Exodus 40:34).\n• As a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night leading the Israelites.\n• As God's glory filling Solomon's Temple.\n• As God dwelling in the sanctuary among the people (Exodus 25:8).\n• As fire on Mount Sinai.","evidence":["Exodus 13:21","Exodus 25:8","Exodus 40:34","1 Kings 8:10–11"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"Give two ways in which Jews today may experience the Shekhinah.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Praying together in a minyan.\n• Studying Torah with others.\n• Praying at the Western Wall in Jerusalem.\n• Welcoming Shabbat.\n• Doing acts of kindness such as visiting the sick.\n• Private prayer and meditation.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 3:6","Talmud Sotah 14a"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah today"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Shekhinah influences Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It encourages communal prayer and study. The rabbis taught that when ten people gather to study Torah, the Shekhinah rests among them. Many Jews therefore attend synagogue to pray with a minyan, or join study groups (chavruta), believing that God is especially present when the community meets.\n\n2. It gives special importance to Jerusalem and the Western Wall. The Shekhinah was believed to dwell in the Holy of Holies of the Temple. Jews face Jerusalem when they pray the Amidah, and many visit the Kotel to pray and leave notes in its cracks, feeling close to God where the Temple once stood. Orthodox Jews also pray for the Shekhinah's return to Zion.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 3:6","Amidah: \"who restores his Shekhinah to Zion\""],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah today"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"Explain two contrasting Jewish understandings of the Shekhinah today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Many Orthodox Jews link the Shekhinah closely to the Temple in Jerusalem. They believe the Shekhinah went into exile with the Jewish people when the Temple was destroyed, and they pray daily for the Temple to be rebuilt and for God's presence to return to Zion, often in the messianic age.\n\n2. Many Reform and Liberal Jews see the Shekhinah as God's closeness felt anywhere: in community, in nature and in ethical action. They do not pray for the Temple sacrifices to be restored, and their prayer books often rephrase those prayers. For them God's presence is found in worship and in working for tikkun olam, not tied to one building.","evidence":["Talmud Megillah 29a","Reform siddur revisions of Temple prayers"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah: diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about the Shekhinah. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. God's presence dwelt in the Tabernacle and the Temple. God commanded, \"Let them make me a sanctuary, that I may dwell among them\" (Exodus 25:8). When the Tabernacle was completed, a cloud covered it and \"the glory of the Lord filled the Tabernacle\" (Exodus 40:34). Later the Holy of Holies in Solomon's Temple was understood as the place where God's presence rested, so the Temple became the centre of Jewish worship.\n\n2. The Shekhinah remains with the Jewish people wherever they are. After the destruction of the Temple, the rabbis taught that the Shekhinah went into exile with Israel (Megillah 29a), so God has not abandoned the people. It is present when Jews pray and study together: Pirkei Avot says that when people sit together and discuss Torah, the Shekhinah is among them. This gives comfort in suffering and makes the synagogue a holy place.","evidence":["Exodus 25:8","Exodus 40:34","Talmud Megillah 29a","Pirkei Avot 3:2, 3:6"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"Explain two reasons why the Shekhinah is important to Jews. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It shows that the transcendent God is also close to people. Although God is beyond the universe, the Shekhinah means God dwells among people. Isaiah heard the angels say, \"The whole earth is full of his glory\" (Isaiah 6:3). This reassures Jews that they can approach God in prayer anywhere, not just in a special place.\n\n2. It comforts Jews through exile and suffering. The rabbis taught that wherever Israel was exiled, the Shekhinah went with them (Megillah 29a). Throughout persecution, including the Holocaust, many Jews have drawn strength from the belief that God shares in their suffering and has not abandoned them. It also gives hope that God's presence will one day be fully restored.","evidence":["Isaiah 6:3","Talmud Megillah 29a"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"\"The Shekhinah can only be experienced in a holy building.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nIn the Tenakh, God's presence is closely linked to sacred buildings. God told Moses, \"Let them make me a sanctuary, that I may dwell among them\" (Exodus 25:8), and the glory of the Lord filled the Tabernacle and later Solomon's Temple (1 Kings 8:10–11). The Holy of Holies was so holy that only the High Priest could enter, and only on Yom Kippur. This suggests that God chose to make the Shekhinah dwell in a particular holy place.\n\nEven today, many Jews feel God's presence most strongly in holy places. The synagogue is called a mikdash me'at, a \"small sanctuary\", which echoes the Temple. The ark containing the Torah scrolls and the ner tamid recall the Temple's holiness. The Western Wall is a place where many feel especially close to God. Orthodox Jews pray for the Temple to be rebuilt so that the Shekhinah may return to Zion, which suggests a link between God's presence and a holy building.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nJewish teaching says clearly that the Shekhinah is not limited to buildings. The rabbis taught that it rests on any group studying Torah together, even two people (Pirkei Avot 3:2), and that it went into exile with the people after the Temple was destroyed (Megillah 29a). If God's presence were tied to a building, Judaism could not have survived almost two thousand years without the Temple.\n\nThe Shekhinah is also found in the home and in ethical action. Shabbat, celebrated mainly at home, is linked with God's presence, and the home is also called a \"small sanctuary\". The Talmud teaches that God visits the sick, so Jews who visit the sick are following God's ways. Many Reform and Liberal Jews stress that God can be experienced in nature, in community and in working for tikkun olam.\n\nFinally, the Tenakh itself shows God appearing outside buildings: in the burning bush, on Mount Sinai and to the prophet Elijah as \"a still small voice\" (1 Kings 19:12). Isaiah says, \"The whole earth is full of his glory.\"\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is not convincing. Holy buildings have been, and still are, important places where Jews feel God's presence, but both the Tenakh and rabbinic teaching show that the Shekhinah can be experienced wherever Jews pray, study, keep Shabbat or act with kindness. That Jewish life continued after the Temple's destruction is strong evidence that the Shekhinah is not confined to one place.","evidence":["Exodus 25:8","1 Kings 8:10–11","1 Kings 19:12","Isaiah 6:3","Pirkei Avot 3:2","Talmud Megillah 29a"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","question":"\"Belief in the Shekhinah is no longer important for Jews because the Temple has been destroyed.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nIn the Tenakh, the Shekhinah was most clearly experienced in the Tabernacle and the Temple, as a cloud and as God's glory filling the building. That Temple was destroyed in 70 CE, the sacrifices ended, and nobody has entered the Holy of Holies since. Some would argue that the vivid, visible presence described in Exodus and 1 Kings belongs to the past, and that modern Judaism concentrates instead on keeping the mitzvot and on ethics.\n\nMany Reform and Liberal Jews do not pray for the Temple to be rebuilt and have changed prayers about restoring the Shekhinah to Zion. For some secular or cultural Jews, the idea of God's dwelling presence plays little part in their identity, which centres on family, festivals and community.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nThe rabbis taught that the destruction of the Temple did not end the Shekhinah's presence. Instead, the Shekhinah went into exile with the people (Megillah 29a). This belief became more important after 70 CE, not less, because it reassured Jews that God had not abandoned them. Through centuries of persecution, and especially after the Holocaust, many Jews found comfort in the idea that God shares their suffering.\n\nThe Shekhinah is now linked with prayer, study and community. Pirkei Avot teaches that when ten people study Torah together, the Shekhinah is present, which helps explain the importance of the minyan and the synagogue as a \"small sanctuary\". Every weekday Orthodox Jews say in the Amidah, \"Blessed are you, Lord, who restores his Shekhinah to Zion,\" so the belief is part of their daily life.\n\nThe belief also shapes ethical life and attitudes to Jerusalem. Jews face Jerusalem to pray, visit the Western Wall, and are taught to imitate God's presence by visiting the sick and comforting mourners. Even Reform Jews who do not want the Temple rebuilt often speak of experiencing God's presence in worship and social action.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is weak. The destruction of the Temple changed how Jews understand the Shekhinah, but it did not make the belief unimportant; arguably it made it more important, since it moved from a building to the people themselves. Orthodox and Reform Jews interpret it differently, but for both it still explains how God remains close, so the stronger view is that the belief is still significant.","evidence":["Talmud Megillah 29a","Pirkei Avot 3:6","Amidah blessing for the return to Zion","Talmud Sotah 14a"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Shekhinah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"What is the Jewish term for 'the world to come'?\nA Gehinnom\nB Olam Ha-Ba\nC Shekhinah\nD Mashiach","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – Olam Ha-Ba. Gehinnom is a place of purification, Shekhinah is the divine presence, and Mashiach is the Messiah.","evidence":["Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"life after death"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"Which one of the following is a Jewish belief about the resurrection of the dead?\nA Souls are reborn in a different body many times\nB The dead will be raised to life at a future time\nC Only animals are resurrected\nD The body is unimportant, so cremation is required","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B. Daniel 12:2 and Maimonides' 13th Principle teach that the dead will be raised in the future, often linked to the messianic age. Rebirth into many bodies (A) is not mainstream Jewish teaching, and Orthodox Jews forbid cremation.","evidence":["Daniel 12:2","Maimonides, 13th Principle"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"resurrection"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"Give two Jewish beliefs about what happens after death.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• The soul returns to God (Ecclesiastes 12:7).\n• God judges each person's life.\n• The righteous go to Gan Eden.\n• Souls may be purified in Gehinnom for up to twelve months.\n• The dead will be resurrected in the messianic age.\n• A person lives on through their good deeds, children and memory (a common Reform view).","evidence":["Ecclesiastes 12:7","Daniel 12:2","Mishnah Eduyot 2:10"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"life after death"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"Give two reasons why Jews may focus more on this life than on life after death.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• The Torah says little about the afterlife.\n• The mitzvot are to be lived in this world.\n• Judaism is a religion of deeds.\n• \"Choose life\" (Deuteronomy 30:19).\n• Pirkei Avot says one hour of good deeds in this world is better than all of the world to come.\n• Nobody can know what happens after death.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 30:19","Pirkei Avot 4:17"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"life after death"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"Explain two contrasting Jewish beliefs about resurrection.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Many Orthodox Jews believe in the bodily resurrection of the dead. It is Maimonides' 13th Principle of Faith, and Daniel 12:2 speaks of those who \"sleep in the dust\" awakening. In the Amidah they praise God \"who gives life to the dead\". Because the body will be raised, they bury the body whole and quickly and do not allow cremation.\n\n2. Many Reform and Liberal Jews do not believe in a physical resurrection. Instead they believe the soul is immortal, or that people live on through their deeds and in the memory of those who loved them. Some Reform prayer books change the Amidah blessing to \"who gives life to all\", and Reform Judaism permits cremation.","evidence":["Daniel 12:2","Amidah, second blessing (Gevurot)","Pittsburgh Platform (1885), Reform rejection of bodily resurrection"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"resurrection"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in judgement after death influences Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It encourages them to keep the mitzvot and live ethically. Jews believe God will judge their lives, so they try to act justly, give tzedakah and show kindness, knowing their choices matter to God. The rabbis compared this world to a corridor before a banqueting hall: people should prepare themselves in the corridor (Pirkei Avot 4:16).\n\n2. It shapes repentance and mourning practices. Many Jews do teshuvah regularly, especially at the High Holy Days, so as to stand right before God. Mourners recite the Kaddish for eleven months after a parent dies. Many believe this helps the soul of the dead, who may spend up to twelve months being purified in Gehinnom before entering Gan Eden.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 4:16","Mishnah Eduyot 2:10"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"judgement"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about judgement after death. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. God judges each soul according to how the person lived. Ecclesiastes 12:7 teaches that \"the spirit returns to God who gave it\", and the following verses say that God will bring every deed into judgement (12:14). Jews believe they will be held to account for keeping the mitzvot and for treating others justly, which gives moral weight to daily choices.\n\n2. Judgement is merciful and usually leads to purification rather than everlasting punishment. Rabbinic teaching (Mishnah Eduyot 2:10) says the judgement of the wicked in Gehinnom lasts twelve months. After this most souls are purified and enter Gan Eden. The Mishnah also teaches that \"all Israel have a share in the world to come\" (Sanhedrin 10:1), and the rabbis taught that the righteous of all nations share in it too, showing that God's judgement is fair and generous.","evidence":["Ecclesiastes 12:7, 12:14","Mishnah Eduyot 2:10","Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1","Tosefta Sanhedrin 13:2"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"judgement"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"Explain two Jewish beliefs about resurrection. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The dead will be raised in the future. Daniel 12:2 teaches that \"many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth will awake, some to everlasting life, others to reproach\". Many Orthodox Jews believe this will happen in the messianic age, and it is the last of Maimonides' Thirteen Principles. It gives hope that death is not the end.\n\n2. Resurrection is praised daily in prayer. The second blessing of the Amidah praises God as the one \"who gives life to the dead\". The Mishnah (Sanhedrin 10:1) warns that a person who denies the resurrection loses their share in the world to come. Belief in the resurrection of the body explains why Orthodox Jews treat the body with great respect after death: they bury it whole, without cremation and usually within a day.","evidence":["Daniel 12:2","Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1","Amidah, second blessing","Ezekiel 37"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"resurrection"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"\"Jews should focus on this life, not on life after death.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Torah itself says very little about the afterlife. Its main concern is how to live in this world: God gives the mitzvot and commands, \"Choose life, so that you and your descendants may live\" (Deuteronomy 30:19). Judaism is often described as a religion of deeds, in which keeping Shabbat, giving tzedakah and acting justly are the centre of religious life.\n\nMany Jews argue that focusing on rewards after death could make people act for selfish reasons. Pirkei Avot (1:3) teaches that one should serve God not like a servant hoping for a reward, but out of love. Pirkei Avot 4:17 even says that one hour of repentance and good deeds in this world is better than all the life of the world to come. Reform and Liberal Jews, many of whom are uncertain about life after death, stress living on through good deeds and working for tikkun olam now.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nBelief in life after death is an important part of traditional Jewish teaching. Daniel 12:2 speaks of the dead awakening, the Mishnah says \"all Israel have a share in the world to come\", and Maimonides made resurrection the 13th Principle of Faith. Orthodox Jews praise God \"who gives life to the dead\" in the Amidah three times a day, so the belief cannot be pushed aside.\n\nBelief in judgement after death also gives this life meaning. If God judges each soul, then moral choices matter, and injustice in this world will ultimately be put right. The same passage of Pirkei Avot compares this world to a corridor leading to a banqueting hall: prepare in the corridor so that you may enter the hall. On this view, focusing on the world to come is a reason to live well now, not a distraction from it.\n\nThe belief also comforts the bereaved and shapes practice: quick burial, no cremation for Orthodox Jews, and the Mourner's Kaddish all reflect beliefs about the soul and resurrection.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is largely right about the emphasis of Judaism, which centres on keeping the mitzvot in this world. It goes too far, however, if it suggests that life after death does not matter. Traditional teaching shows that the two belong together: the hope of Olam Ha-Ba and the reality of judgement are reasons to live faithfully now. The strongest conclusion is that Jews should focus on this life because of, not instead of, their belief in what comes after.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 30:19","Pirkei Avot 1:3, 4:16–17","Daniel 12:2","Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"life after death"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","question":"\"Belief in resurrection is essential for Jews.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nResurrection is part of traditional Jewish belief. Maimonides included it as the 13th of his Principles of Faith, and the Mishnah (Sanhedrin 10:1) states that a person who denies the resurrection of the dead has no share in the world to come. That is a very strong statement that the belief is not optional.\n\nIt is also part of daily worship. The second blessing of the Amidah praises God four times as the one who gives life to the dead, and Orthodox Jews say it at every service. The Tenakh supports it: Daniel 12:2 speaks of those who \"sleep in the dust\" awakening, and Ezekiel's vision of dry bones coming to life (Ezekiel 37) gives hope of renewal. For Orthodox Jews the belief also shapes practice: they bury the body whole, forbid cremation, and link resurrection to the coming of the Messiah.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nReform Judaism has long rejected bodily resurrection. The Pittsburgh Platform of 1885 described the soul as immortal but rejected bodily resurrection, and some Reform prayer books offer \"who gives life to all\" in the Amidah. Reform Jews remain fully Jewish, so the belief cannot be essential to being Jewish in practice.\n\nThe Torah, the most authoritative part of the Tenakh, does not clearly teach resurrection; the clearest text, Daniel, is from a much later book. Judaism is more concerned with deeds than with creeds, and a Jew is defined by birth or conversion, not by signing up to a list of beliefs. Many Jews, Orthodox included, admit uncertainty about what resurrection will be like and focus on living by the mitzvot now. Others prefer to believe in the immortality of the soul, or in living on through their children and their good deeds.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nWhether the statement is true depends on which Jewish perspective is taken. For Orthodox Jews, following the Mishnah and Maimonides, belief in resurrection is a principle of faith and therefore essential. For Reform and Liberal Jews it is not, and their Jewish identity rests on covenant, ethics and community. Since Judaism places more weight on practice than on a fixed creed, the fairer conclusion is that resurrection is an important traditional belief but not essential for all Jews today.","evidence":["Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1","Maimonides, 13th Principle","Daniel 12:2","Ezekiel 37","Pittsburgh Platform 1885"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"resurrection"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"What does the Hebrew word Mashiach mean?\nA Teacher\nB Anointed one\nC Prophet\nD Saviour of the world","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – anointed one. Kings and priests in ancient Israel were anointed with oil to show that God had chosen them, and the Messiah is expected to be an anointed king descended from David.","evidence":["1 Samuel 16:13 (David anointed)"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Messiah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"From whose family line do many Jews believe the Messiah will come?\nA Moses\nB Abraham's son Ishmael\nC King David\nD Aaron the priest","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C – King David. Isaiah 11:1 speaks of \"a shoot from the stump of Jesse\" (David's father), and Jeremiah 23:5 of a \"righteous branch\" raised up for David.","evidence":["Isaiah 11:1","Jeremiah 23:5"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Messiah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"Give two things Jews traditionally believe the Messiah will do.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Bring world peace.\n• Gather Jews from exile to Israel.\n• Rebuild the Temple in Jerusalem.\n• Rule justly as a king of David's line.\n• Bring all people to know the one God.\n• Usher in the resurrection of the dead or the messianic age.","evidence":["Isaiah 2:4","Isaiah 11:1–9"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"role of the Messiah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"Give two different Jewish views about the Messiah.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• A personal human Messiah from David's line will come (Orthodox).\n• There will be a messianic age brought about by human effort rather than one person (Reform).\n• The founding of Israel is the beginning of redemption (Religious Zionist).\n• Only the Messiah may establish a Jewish state (some strictly Orthodox groups).\n• The Messiah is a symbol of hope rather than a literal figure.","evidence":["Maimonides, 12th Principle","Prayer for the State of Israel"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"Messiah: diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"Explain two contrasting Jewish beliefs about the Messiah.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Orthodox Jews believe in a personal Messiah, a human descendant of King David, who will come to rebuild the Temple, gather the exiles and bring peace. They pray daily for his coming in the Amidah and affirm Maimonides' 12th Principle that they will wait for him every day, even if he delays.\n\n2. Many Reform and Liberal Jews do not expect one individual Messiah. They believe in a messianic age of peace and justice that humanity must bring about by working together for tikkun olam. Some Reform prayer books replace the word \"redeemer\" with \"redemption\" to show this. For them the messianic idea is a call to action rather than a wait for a saviour.","evidence":["Maimonides, 12th Principle","Isaiah 11:1–9","Reform siddur: ge'ulah (redemption) for go'el (redeemer)"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Messiah: diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Messiah influences Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It gives hope in suffering. Belief that God will one day bring a time of peace has strengthened Jews through persecution; some Jews in the Nazi camps sang \"Ani Ma'amin\", Maimonides' declaration of faith in the Messiah's coming. Today the belief gives hope that injustice and conflict will not last for ever.\n\n2. It motivates action and prayer. Many Jews believe that keeping the mitzvot and acting justly helps prepare the world for the messianic age, so they work for tikkun olam and support charities. It is also expressed in worship: at the Pesach seder a cup is poured for Elijah, the herald of the Messiah, and the door is opened for him, and the seder ends with \"Next year in Jerusalem\".","evidence":["Ani Ma'amin (based on Maimonides' 12th Principle)","Malachi 3:23 (Elijah before the day of the Lord)"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Messiah today"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about the role of the Messiah. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The Messiah will bring universal peace. Isaiah prophesies that in that time, \"they shall beat their swords into ploughshares… nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more\" (Isaiah 2:4). Jews believe the Messiah's age will end war and hatred, and Isaiah 11:6 pictures even the wolf living with the lamb.\n\n2. The Messiah will restore the Jewish people and lead the world to God. Traditional teaching, summed up by Maimonides in the Mishneh Torah, says the Messiah will gather the exiles back to the land of Israel and rebuild the Temple in Jerusalem. Isaiah 11:9 says \"the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the Lord\". This is why Jews say the Messiah has not yet come: these tasks have not yet been fulfilled.","evidence":["Isaiah 2:4","Isaiah 11:6–9","Maimonides, Mishneh Torah, Laws of Kings 11"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"role of the Messiah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"Explain two Jewish beliefs about the nature of the Messiah. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The Messiah will be a human being, not God. Jews believe God is one and cannot become human (Deuteronomy 6:4), so the Messiah will be an ordinary mortal chosen and anointed by God, like the kings of Israel. Maimonides wrote that the Messiah will be a king who studies Torah and observes the mitzvot, and he will be judged by whether he achieves the messianic tasks.\n\n2. The Messiah will be descended from King David and filled with God's spirit. Isaiah 11:1–2 describes \"a shoot from the stump of Jesse\" on whom \"the spirit of the Lord shall rest – a spirit of wisdom and understanding\". So the Messiah is expected to be a wise, just and righteous ruler from David's family, which links the messianic hope to God's promises to David.","evidence":["Isaiah 11:1–2","Jeremiah 23:5","Deuteronomy 6:4","Maimonides, Laws of Kings 11:4"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"nature of the Messiah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"\"Belief in the Messiah is the most important Jewish belief.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nBelief in the Messiah is part of traditional Jewish faith and daily worship. Maimonides made it the 12th Principle of Faith: \"though he may tarry, I will wait daily for his coming\". Orthodox Jews pray for the Messiah and the rebuilding of Jerusalem in the Amidah three times a day. A belief that Jews pray about every day is clearly very important.\n\nThe Messiah gives purpose and direction to Jewish history. The prophets promise a future of peace, when nations \"beat their swords into ploughshares\" (Isaiah 2:4) and the exiles return. This hope sustained Jews through exile and persecution, including the Holocaust, when some went to their deaths singing \"Ani Ma'amin\". For many Religious Zionists the founding of Israel in 1948 is part of this messianic story, which shows the belief still shapes events today.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nMany Jews would say that belief in one God is far more important. The Shema, \"the Lord is One\", is the foundation of Judaism; the Messiah is only God's agent. Without the one God there could be no Messiah at all, so belief in God must come first.\n\nOthers argue that the covenant and the mitzvot matter more for daily life. Judaism focuses on how one lives now, keeping Shabbat, kashrut and tzedakah, rather than speculating about the future. A well-known teaching attributed to Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai says that if you are planting a tree and are told the Messiah has come, finish planting the tree first and then go to greet him. Present duties come before messianic hopes.\n\nReform and Liberal Jews mostly do not believe in a personal Messiah at all. They speak of a messianic age built through human effort. If many committed Jews do not hold the belief, it cannot be the most important one.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nBelief in the Messiah is important, especially for Orthodox Jews, as a source of hope and a vision of the future. It is not the most important belief, however: it depends on the more basic belief in one God, and Jewish life centres on keeping the covenant in the present. The fact that Reform Jews interpret it so differently also suggests that it is less central than belief in God or the Torah.","evidence":["Maimonides, 12th Principle","Isaiah 2:4","Deuteronomy 6:4","Avot de-Rabbi Natan (version B), ch. 31 – planting a tree before greeting the Messiah"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Messiah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The nature and role of the Messiah","question":"\"Humans, not a Messiah, must bring peace to the world.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nMany Reform and Liberal Jews agree with this statement. They do not expect one individual saviour; they speak of a messianic age that humanity creates through justice and compassion. The idea of tikkun olam, healing or repairing the world, teaches that humans are God's partners in perfecting creation. Waiting passively for a Messiah could make people neglect their duty to fight injustice now.\n\nJewish teaching repeatedly calls people to act. Micah 6:8 asks what God requires: \"to do justice, to love kindness and to walk humbly with your God\". Pirkei Avot 2:16 teaches, \"It is not your duty to finish the work, but neither are you free to desist from it.\" Jews are commanded to \"seek peace and pursue it\" (Psalm 34:14). Even traditional Jews accept that humans must work for peace by keeping the mitzvot, giving tzedakah and practising gemilut chasadim (kindness).\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOrthodox Jews believe that God has promised to send the Messiah, a descendant of David, who will bring lasting peace. The prophets describe a transformation of the world that seems beyond human ability: \"the wolf shall live with the lamb\" (Isaiah 11:6) and nations \"shall not learn war any more\" (Isaiah 2:4). Human history, with its repeated wars, suggests that people alone cannot achieve this, and only God working through the Messiah can.\n\nMaimonides' 12th Principle commits Jews to waiting for the Messiah every day. Some strictly Orthodox groups go further and believe that humans should not try to force the redemption, for example by founding a Jewish state, before the Messiah comes.\n\nMany traditional Jews would combine the two views: human righteousness prepares the way, and the Talmud suggests the Messiah could come in a generation that is wholly deserving. On this view, human effort and the Messiah work together rather than competing.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is persuasive in that every Jewish group agrees humans have a duty to work for peace now; neither Micah nor Pirkei Avot lets anyone off. Whether humans alone can complete the task is where views divide. Orthodox Judaism holds that final peace needs God's Messiah, while Reform Judaism places the task in human hands. The most balanced conclusion is that humans must bring peace, but traditional Jews would add that the task will finally be completed by God.","evidence":["Micah 6:8","Pirkei Avot 2:16","Psalm 34:14","Isaiah 2:4, 11:6","Talmud Sanhedrin 98a"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Messiah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"Which one of the following did God promise Abram in Genesis 12:1–3?\nA That he would become king of Egypt\nB That he would become a great nation\nC That he would build the Temple\nD That he would receive the Ten Commandments","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – a great nation. In Genesis 12:2 God says, \"I will make of you a great nation, and I will bless you.\" Moses, not Abraham, received the Ten Commandments, and Solomon built the Temple.","evidence":["Genesis 12:2"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Covenant with Abraham"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"What was the sign of the covenant God gave Abraham in Genesis 17?\nA The rainbow\nB Shabbat\nC Circumcision\nD The tefillin","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C – circumcision (brit milah) of every male at eight days old (Genesis 17:10–12). The rainbow was the sign of the covenant with Noah, and Shabbat is called a sign of the Sinai covenant.","evidence":["Genesis 17:10–12"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Covenant with Abraham"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"Give two promises God made to Abraham.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Land (\"the land that I will show you\" or Canaan).\n• Many descendants or a great nation.\n• Blessing.\n• A great name.\n• All the families of the earth will be blessed through him.\n• God will be their God for ever (Genesis 17:7).","evidence":["Genesis 12:1–3","Genesis 15:5","Genesis 17:7–8"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"Covenant with Abraham"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"Give two ways in which the Covenant with Abraham is important for Jews today.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Boys are circumcised at brit milah as a sign of the covenant.\n• It gives Jews a special relationship with God.\n• It is the basis of the Jewish link to the land of Israel.\n• Abraham is a role model of faith and obedience.\n• It gives Jews a duty to be a blessing to others.\n• It is the origin of the Jewish people.","evidence":["Genesis 12:1–3","Genesis 17:10"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"Covenant today"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"Explain two ways in which the Covenant with Abraham influences Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Through brit milah. God told Abraham that circumcision would be \"the sign of the covenant\" in every generation (Genesis 17:10–12). Jewish parents today have their baby sons circumcised on the eighth day in a ceremony, welcoming the child into the covenant that began with Abraham. Orthodox and most Reform families keep this practice.\n\n2. Through their relationship with the land of Israel. God promised Abraham \"the land that I will show you\", the land of Canaan. Many Jews feel a deep connection to Israel as the promised land: they pray facing Jerusalem, visit it, support Israeli charities, and some make aliyah (emigrate) there under the Law of Return. Many see this connection as part of God's covenant promise.","evidence":["Genesis 17:10–12","Genesis 12:1, 12:7","Law of Return (1950)"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Covenant today"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"Explain two contrasting Jewish views about the importance of the promised land today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Many Orthodox and Religious Zionist Jews believe the modern State of Israel is the fulfilment of God's covenant promise to Abraham that his descendants would inherit the land (Genesis 17:8). They see living in or supporting Israel as a religious duty, and some regard the state as \"the first flowering of our redemption\".\n\n2. Some strictly Orthodox groups, such as Neturei Karta, believe that the Jewish return to the land should happen only when the Messiah comes. They therefore oppose a secular Jewish state, while still valuing the holiness of the land. Many Reform Jews take a different approach: they support Israel, but they emphasise the universal part of the covenant, that \"all the families of the earth\" will be blessed, and see Jewish life outside Israel, for example in Britain, as fully valid.","evidence":["Genesis 17:8","Genesis 12:3","Prayer for the State of Israel"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"promised land"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"Explain two promises that God made to Abraham in the covenant. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The promise of land. God told Abram, \"Go forth from your land… to the land that I will show you\" (Genesis 12:1), and later promised \"all the land of Canaan, for an everlasting possession\" (Genesis 17:8). This is the origin of the Jewish belief that Eretz Yisrael is the promised land, which is why Jews pray facing Jerusalem.\n\n2. The promise of a great nation and blessing. God said, \"I will make of you a great nation… and all the families of the earth shall be blessed through you\" (Genesis 12:2–3). Although Abraham and Sarah were old and childless, God promised descendants as many as the stars (Genesis 15:5), fulfilled through Isaac and Jacob. Jews see themselves as these descendants, with a responsibility to be a blessing to the wider world.","evidence":["Genesis 12:1–3","Genesis 15:5","Genesis 17:8"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"Covenant with Abraham"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"Explain two ways in which Abraham showed his part in the covenant. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Faith and obedience in leaving his home. When God commanded, \"Go forth from your land, from your birthplace and from your father's house\" (Genesis 12:1), Abram left everything without knowing where he was going. Jews see this as the model of trust in God, and Genesis 15:6 says Abraham \"believed in the Lord\".\n\n2. Accepting circumcision and passing faith to his descendants. In Genesis 17 Abraham circumcised himself and all the males of his household as the sign of the covenant. He was later willing even to offer his son Isaac (the Akedah, Genesis 22), showing complete obedience; God stopped him and renewed the promises. Jews continue Abraham's side of the covenant through brit milah and by bringing up children to follow God.","evidence":["Genesis 12:1","Genesis 15:6","Genesis 17:23–24","Genesis 22 (the Akedah)"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"Abraham"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"\"The Covenant with Abraham is the most important covenant for Jews.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Covenant with Abraham is the beginning of the Jewish people. In Genesis 12:1–3 God calls Abram, promises him land, a great nation and blessing, and Abram responds with faith. Without this covenant there would be no descendants of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and so no Israelites at Sinai. Jews call him Avraham Avinu, \"Abraham our father\", which shows how basic he is to their identity.\n\nThe covenant is still marked physically today. God told Abraham that circumcision would be an \"everlasting covenant\" (Genesis 17:13), and Jewish boys are still circumcised at eight days old. The promise of land also links Jews to Israel today: many see the 1948 State of Israel and the Law of Return as connected to the promise of the land of Canaan.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nMany Jews would argue that the Covenant at Sinai is more important. At Sinai God gave the Torah and the Ten Commandments to the whole people, not one family, and Israel became \"a kingdom of priests and a holy nation\" (Exodus 19:6). The 613 mitzvot, which shape every part of Jewish life from Shabbat to kashrut, come from Sinai. Judaism is a way of life based on the Torah, so the covenant that gave the Torah could be seen as the most important.\n\nIt can also be argued that the covenants should not be ranked, because they form one developing relationship. Sinai fulfils the promises to Abraham: his descendants become a nation, are given laws, and are led towards the promised land. Reform Jews may stress the ethical and universal side of both covenants rather than choosing between them.\n\nSome point out that the land promise is controversial in modern politics, while the moral law of Sinai unites Jews more widely.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe Covenant with Abraham is the foundation, because it creates the people and the promise. Sinai, however, gives that people its way of life through the Torah, and most Jews today experience their faith through keeping the mitzvot. The strongest conclusion is that Abraham's covenant is the most important in origin but the Sinai covenant is the most important in practice, and that Jews see them as two stages of one covenant rather than as rivals.","evidence":["Genesis 12:1–3","Genesis 17:13","Exodus 19:5–6","Law of Return (1950)"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Covenant with Abraham"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","question":"\"The promised land is still the most important part of God's covenant with the Jews.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe land is central to the promises in Genesis. God's first words to Abram are a command to go \"to the land that I will show you\" (Genesis 12:1), and the land is promised again and again as \"an everlasting possession\" (Genesis 17:8). For centuries of exile Jews prayed facing Jerusalem, mourned the loss of the Temple on Tisha B'Av, and ended the seder with \"Next year in Jerusalem\".\n\nFor many Jews the land is still vital today. The founding of the State of Israel in 1948 gave Jews a homeland and a place of safety after the Holocaust, and the Law of Return allows any Jew to settle there. Religious Zionists see Israel as the start of redemption, and many British Jews visit, study there or make aliyah. Many mitzvot, such as some agricultural laws, can only be kept in the land itself.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nMany Jews would argue that the Torah and the mitzvot are more important than the land. Judaism survived nearly 2,000 years without a Jewish state, because the covenant was carried in the Torah, the synagogue and the home. The rabbis taught that the Shekhinah went into exile with the people, so the relationship with God is not dependent on land.\n\nReform Judaism has historically placed less emphasis on the return to the land. It stresses the ethical mission of being \"a blessing\" to \"all the families of the earth\" (Genesis 12:3) wherever Jews live. Some strictly Orthodox Jews believe the land should be restored only by the Messiah, and so they see the modern state as premature. Many Jews also feel that the present Israeli–Palestinian conflict makes it important not to make the land the whole of the covenant, and they emphasise justice and peace.\n\nOthers would say the relationship with God itself, \"I will be your God\" (Genesis 17:7), is the heart of the covenant, and that the land is one of several gifts within it.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe land is a very important part of the covenant, and for many Orthodox and Zionist Jews it is central. However, Jewish history shows that the covenant survived and flourished without the land, through Torah and mitzvot. The more convincing view is that the relationship with God and the keeping of the Torah are the heart of the covenant, and the land is a precious but not the most important part of it.","evidence":["Genesis 12:1–3","Genesis 17:7–8","Talmud Megillah 29a","Law of Return (1950)"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"promised land"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"Which festival celebrates the giving of the Torah at Mount Sinai?\nA Pesach\nB Shavuot\nC Rosh Hashanah\nD Yom Kippur","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – Shavuot, held fifty days after the second day of Pesach, celebrates the giving of the Torah at Sinai. The Ten Commandments are read in synagogue that day.","evidence":["Exodus 19–20","Leviticus 23:15–21"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Covenant at Sinai"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"Which one of the following is one of the Ten Commandments in Exodus 20?\nA Do not eat pork\nB Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy\nC Pray three times a day\nD Give a tenth of your income to charity","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B. \"Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy\" (Exodus 20:8) is the fourth commandment in the Jewish numbering. The others are Jewish practices, but they are not among the Ten Commandments.","evidence":["Exodus 20:8"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Ten Commandments"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"Give two of the Ten Commandments about relationships between people.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Honour your father and your mother.\n• Do not murder.\n• Do not commit adultery.\n• Do not steal.\n• Do not bear false witness.\n• Do not covet.","evidence":["Exodus 20:12–17"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"Ten Commandments"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"Give two roles of Moses in the Covenant at Sinai.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• He led the Israelites out of Egypt to Sinai.\n• He received the Ten Commandments and the Torah from God.\n• He was the mediator between God and the people.\n• He was a prophet.\n• He taught the law to the people.\n• He pleaded for the people after the golden calf.","evidence":["Exodus 19:3","Exodus 32:11–14","Deuteronomy 34:10"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"Moses"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"Explain two ways in which the Ten Commandments influence Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. They guide Jewish worship of God. The opening commandments, to have no other gods, make no idols and not misuse God's name, mean that Jews worship only one God, have no images in synagogues and treat God's name with respect; many write \"G-d\". The command to remember the Sabbath leads Jews to keep Shabbat each week with candles, Kiddush, synagogue worship and rest.\n\n2. They shape how Jews treat other people. Commands such as \"Do not murder\", \"Do not steal\" and \"Do not bear false witness\" guide Jewish ethics in daily life, for example honesty at work, respect for life and faithfulness in marriage. \"Honour your father and your mother\" encourages Jews to care for elderly parents.","evidence":["Exodus 20:2–17"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Ten Commandments"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"Explain two contrasting Jewish beliefs about the Torah given at Sinai.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Orthodox Jews believe God revealed the whole Torah, both written and oral, to Moses at Sinai. It is the unchanging word of God (Torah min ha-shamayim, \"Torah from heaven\"), so all 613 mitzvot are binding and must be kept as interpreted by halakhah.\n\n2. Reform Jews believe the Torah was inspired by God but written by human beings over many centuries, reflecting their times. Revelation is \"progressive\": it continues as each generation understands God's will more deeply. So Reform Jews keep the ethical laws as binding but make informed choices about ritual laws, and they have made changes such as equal roles for women.","evidence":["Exodus 24:12","Pirkei Avot 1:1 (\"Moses received the Torah from Sinai\")"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Torah from Sinai"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"Explain two reasons why the Covenant at Sinai is important for Jews. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It made the Israelites God's people with a special role. God promised that if they kept the covenant they would be \"my treasured possession… a kingdom of priests and a holy nation\" (Exodus 19:5–6). Jews believe they were chosen not for privilege but for responsibility: to live by God's law and be an example to the world.\n\n2. It gave the Torah and the mitzvot. At Sinai God gave the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:1–17), and tradition holds that the 613 commandments also come from Sinai. The people replied \"we will do and we will understand\" (Exodus 24:7). This means the whole of Jewish life, including Shabbat, kashrut, prayer and ethics, rests on the Sinai covenant, and it is celebrated every year at Shavuot.","evidence":["Exodus 19:5–6","Exodus 20:1–17","Exodus 24:7"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"Covenant at Sinai"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about the role of Moses. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Moses was the greatest prophet. The Torah says, \"There has not arisen a prophet since in Israel like Moses, whom the Lord knew face to face\" (Deuteronomy 34:10). Maimonides' 7th Principle teaches that Moses' prophecy was greater than any other, which gives the Torah he received unique authority.\n\n2. Moses was the lawgiver and teacher who passed on the Torah. Pirkei Avot 1:1 begins, \"Moses received the Torah from Sinai and handed it on to Joshua\", tracing a chain of tradition down to the rabbis. That is why Jews call him Moshe Rabbenu, \"Moses our teacher\". He also acted as mediator, pleading with God to forgive the people after the golden calf (Exodus 32), which shows his care for the people.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 34:10","Pirkei Avot 1:1","Exodus 32:11–14"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"Moses"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"\"The Ten Commandments are the most important part of the Torah.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Ten Commandments were given directly by God to the whole people at Sinai, amid thunder and fire (Exodus 19–20), and written on stone tablets \"by the finger of God\". No other part of the Torah was given in such a dramatic and public way. They were kept in the Ark of the Covenant, the holiest object in the Temple, which shows their status.\n\nThey also summarise the whole Torah. The tenth-century scholar Saadia Gaon argued that all 613 mitzvot can be grouped under the Ten Commandments. They cover both duties to God (one God, no idols, Shabbat) and duties to people (no murder, theft or lying), the two halves of Jewish ethics. Many synagogues display the two tablets above the ark, and congregations stand when they are read, which reflects how highly they are regarded.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nJudaism teaches that all 613 mitzvot come from God and are equally binding. Pirkei Avot 2:1 says one should be as careful with a \"minor\" commandment as with a \"major\" one, because we do not know their reward. In ancient times the rabbis actually stopped the daily public reading of the Ten Commandments, which had been part of Temple worship, so that no one could claim that only these ten were given at Sinai (Talmud Berakhot 12a).\n\nOther passages could be seen as more central. The Shema (Deuteronomy 6:4–9) is recited twice daily and declares God's oneness. Hillel summed up the Torah as \"What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour\" (Shabbat 31a), and Rabbi Akiva called \"Love your neighbour as yourself\" (Leviticus 19:18) a great principle of the Torah. Neither of these is one of the Ten Commandments.\n\nA Reform Jew might emphasise the ethical commandments as the lasting core of the Torah, while an Orthodox Jew would insist that the ritual laws are equally God's will.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe Ten Commandments have a special place, given publicly at Sinai and summing up the main areas of Jewish duty. However, Jewish teaching deliberately avoids treating them as more important than the other commandments, because the whole Torah is from God. The more convincing view is that the Ten Commandments are a powerful summary of the Torah rather than its most important part.","evidence":["Exodus 20:1–17","Exodus 31:18","Pirkei Avot 2:1","Talmud Berakhot 12a","Leviticus 19:18","Talmud Shabbat 31a"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Ten Commandments"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","question":"\"Moses is the most important figure in Judaism.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nMoses received the Torah, which is the foundation of Jewish life. Pirkei Avot opens, \"Moses received the Torah from Sinai\", and all later Jewish law and teaching traces its authority back through him. Without Moses there would be no Ten Commandments, no 613 mitzvot and no Sinai covenant as Jews know it.\n\nThe Torah itself gives Moses unique status: \"There has not arisen a prophet since in Israel like Moses, whom the Lord knew face to face\" (Deuteronomy 34:10). Maimonides made this one of his Thirteen Principles. Moses also led the people out of slavery in Egypt, which Jews remember every year at Pesach, and he interceded for them after the golden calf. Jews call him Moshe Rabbenu, \"our teacher\", a title given to no one else in the same way.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nAbraham could be considered more important, because he was the first to make a covenant with God and is the father of the Jewish people (Genesis 12:1–3). Without Abraham there would be no Israelites for Moses to lead. Jews call him Avraham Avinu, \"our father\", and the covenant of circumcision goes back to him.\n\nInterestingly, Moses is hardly mentioned in the Pesach Haggadah. Many explain this as a deliberate choice to stress that God, not a human being, freed the Israelites. Judaism avoids treating any human as an object of worship. The Torah also says that Moses' burial place is unknown (Deuteronomy 34:6), which many see as a way to stop people making it a shrine. The most important \"figure\" in Judaism is therefore God.\n\nSome would also argue that it is the Torah itself, or the whole people, that matters most, since the covenant was made with all Israel, past and future. Others would point to King David, from whom the Messiah is expected to descend.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nMoses is arguably the most important human figure in Judaism, because the Torah, the centre of Jewish life, came through him and he is the model teacher and prophet. However, Judaism deliberately keeps the focus on God and the Torah rather than on any person, and Abraham has an equal claim as the founder of the covenant. So the statement is partly true: Moses is the greatest prophet, but not more important than God or the Torah he received.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 1:1","Deuteronomy 34:6, 34:10","Genesis 12:1–3","Maimonides, 7th Principle","Pesach Haggadah"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Moses"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"What is the meaning of tikkun olam?\nA Day of atonement\nB Healing or repairing the world\nC The written law\nD Saving a life","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – healing or repairing the world: working, as God's partners, to make the world better through justice, social action and care for creation. Saving a life is pikuach nefesh.","evidence":["Mishnah Gittin 4:2","Aleinu prayer"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"tikkun olam"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"According to Maimonides, which one of the following is the highest level of charity?\nA Giving unwillingly\nB Giving after being asked\nC Helping someone become self-sufficient\nD Giving less than you should, but cheerfully","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C. Maimonides' highest level of tzedakah is helping a person become independent, for example with a loan, a partnership or a job, so they no longer need charity. Giving unwillingly is the lowest.","evidence":["Maimonides, Mishneh Torah, Laws of Gifts to the Poor 10:7–14"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"tzedakah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"Give two examples of gemilut chasadim (acts of loving-kindness).","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Visiting the sick (bikur cholim).\n• Comforting mourners.\n• Welcoming guests (hachnasat orchim).\n• Helping at a funeral or burial.\n• Helping a bride and groom.\n• Lending without interest.\n• Volunteering for others.","evidence":["Talmud Sukkah 49b","Genesis 18:1–8"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"kindness"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"Give two ways in which Jews today put tzedakah into practice.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Giving about 10% of income (ma'aser).\n• Putting coins in a tzedakah box, e.g. before Shabbat.\n• Donating to charities such as World Jewish Relief or Jewish Care.\n• Supporting food banks.\n• Giving extra before Yom Kippur or at Purim (matanot la'evyonim).\n• Giving interest-free loans (gemach).","evidence":["Deuteronomy 15:7–8","Leviticus 19:9–10"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"tzedakah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in justice influences Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Campaigning for fairness and human rights. The Torah commands \"Justice, justice you shall pursue\" (Deuteronomy 16:20), so many Jews campaign against racism, poverty and injustice. For example, some volunteer with refugee organisations, remembering that Jews themselves were once \"strangers in the land of Egypt\".\n\n2. Treating giving as a duty, not a choice. The word for charity, tzedakah, comes from tzedek, justice, so Jews see helping the poor as putting right an injustice rather than as an optional kindness. Many give a tenth of their income and support charities such as Jewish Care or food banks, believing that the poor have a right to support.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 16:20","Deuteronomy 10:19"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"justice"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"Explain two ways in which Jews today try to practise tikkun olam.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Social action and volunteering. Many Jews, especially in Reform and Liberal communities, take part in Mitzvah Day each November, volunteering at hospitals, care homes, food banks and parks. They see themselves as God's partners in repairing the world, as Pirkei Avot says: \"It is not your duty to finish the work, but neither are you free to desist from it.\"\n\n2. Caring for the environment. Because the world is God's creation and humans must \"work it and keep it\" (Genesis 2:15), many Jews work for environmental protection, for example through EcoSynagogue in the UK, reducing waste and planting trees at Tu Bishvat. They see this as healing God's world for future generations.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 2:16","Genesis 2:15"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"tikkun olam"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about charity (tzedakah). Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Charity is a duty of justice. Tzedakah comes from tzedek, justice. The Torah commands, \"Do not harden your heart or shut your hand against your poor brother… open your hand to him\" (Deuteronomy 15:7–8), and farmers had to leave the corners of their fields for the poor (Leviticus 19:9–10). So Jews see giving as an obligation, often around a tenth of their income, rather than a voluntary act.\n\n2. The way you give matters. Maimonides taught eight levels of tzedakah. The highest is helping someone become self-sufficient, for example through a job or a loan; next is giving anonymously so the receiver keeps their dignity; the lowest is giving grudgingly. This encourages Jews to give in ways that respect the poor, for example through interest-free loan societies (gemachs).","evidence":["Deuteronomy 15:7–8","Leviticus 19:9–10","Maimonides, Laws of Gifts to the Poor 10"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"tzedakah"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about kindness to others. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Jews must love their neighbour. The Torah commands, \"Love your neighbour as yourself\" (Leviticus 19:18). Hillel summed up the Torah as \"What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour\" (Talmud Shabbat 31a). So kindness is not an extra but the heart of keeping the Torah.\n\n2. Acts of loving-kindness (gemilut chasadim) are one of the pillars of the world. Pirkei Avot 1:2 says the world stands on Torah, worship and acts of loving-kindness. The Talmud says these acts are greater than charity because they can be done for rich and poor, for the living and the dead, and with one's own body (Sukkah 49b). Jews therefore visit the sick, comfort mourners and welcome guests, following Abraham's hospitality to strangers (Genesis 18).","evidence":["Leviticus 19:18","Talmud Shabbat 31a","Pirkei Avot 1:2","Talmud Sukkah 49b","Genesis 18"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"kindness"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"\"Tikkun olam is the most important moral principle for Jews today.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nTikkun olam, healing the world, sums up the purpose of Jewish moral life: working as God's partners to perfect creation. The Aleinu prayer, recited at the end of every service, looks forward to the world being perfected under God's kingship. Pirkei Avot 2:16 says, \"It is not your duty to finish the work, but neither are you free to desist from it,\" which shows that Jews must keep working to improve the world.\n\nIt also includes the other principles. Pursuing justice, giving tzedakah and acts of kindness can all be seen as ways of repairing the world. In today's world of climate change, poverty and conflict, many Jews, especially in Reform and Liberal communities, see tikkun olam as the central Jewish contribution to society, shown in projects like Mitzvah Day and EcoSynagogue. It also connects Jews to people of other faiths working for the common good.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nSome would argue that justice (tzedek) is more important. The Torah repeats the word, \"Justice, justice you shall pursue\" (Deuteronomy 16:20), and the prophets constantly demand it (Amos 5:24, \"let justice roll down like waters\"). Justice is based on God's own nature as Judge. Tikkun olam as a broad social idea is relatively modern, whereas justice is a direct command of the Torah.\n\nOthers would say kindness, or love of neighbour, is most important. Hillel summed up the whole Torah as \"What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour\", and Rabbi Akiva called \"Love your neighbour as yourself\" a great principle of the Torah. Pirkei Avot lists acts of loving-kindness as one of the three pillars of the world.\n\nMany Orthodox Jews would also warn against picking one principle. They might argue that tikkun olam, if separated from keeping the mitzvot, can become vague, and that the world is repaired mainly by Jews keeping the whole Torah, ritual and ethical, faithfully.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nTikkun olam is a powerful and popular way of expressing Jewish ethics today, and it links individual actions to a larger purpose. However, it depends on more basic, clearly commanded principles, especially justice and loving one's neighbour, which give it its content. The strongest conclusion is that tikkun olam is the goal while justice, charity and kindness are the means, so it is not more important than those principles but brings them together.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 2:16","Aleinu prayer","Deuteronomy 16:20","Amos 5:24","Leviticus 19:18","Talmud Shabbat 31a"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"tikkun olam"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","question":"\"Acts of kindness are more important than giving money to charity.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Talmud explicitly says that gemilut chasadim is greater than tzedakah in three ways (Sukkah 49b): charity is given with money, but kindness can be given with one's whole self; charity is only for the poor, but kindness is for rich and poor; and charity is only for the living, but kindness is for the living and the dead, for example by attending a funeral. So according to rabbinic teaching, the statement is correct.\n\nKindness involves personal relationship. Visiting a lonely, sick person or comforting a mourner during shiva gives something money cannot buy: time, presence and dignity. Pirkei Avot 1:2 names acts of loving-kindness as one of the three things on which the world stands. Abraham, who ran to welcome three strangers (Genesis 18), is a model of personal kindness, not just generosity.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nTzedakah is a clear commandment of justice. The Torah orders Jews to \"open your hand\" to the poor (Deuteronomy 15:8), and the word tzedakah comes from tzedek, justice. Money can meet basic needs on a scale that individual kindness cannot: food, housing and medical care. Charities like World Jewish Relief and Jewish Care can help thousands of people, and a kind visit cannot replace a meal for someone who is starving.\n\nMaimonides' highest level of tzedakah, helping someone become self-sufficient, can change a person's whole life, and it combines money with personal involvement. The rabbis also said that tzedakah is equal to all the other commandments (Bava Batra 9a). For many Jews the two are therefore of equal weight.\n\nIt can also be argued that the question sets up a false choice: Judaism expects both. A Jew is required to give tzedakah and to perform gemilut chasadim, and the High Holy Day liturgy names tzedakah alongside repentance and prayer.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe statement has strong support from the Talmud, which states that kindness is greater because it is broader and more personal. However, Judaism treats both as duties, and in situations of real need, money given as tzedakah may do more good. The best conclusion is that kindness is greater in its scope, as the rabbis taught, but Jews are not free to choose one over the other, since true kindness includes giving.","evidence":["Talmud Sukkah 49b","Pirkei Avot 1:2","Deuteronomy 15:8","Talmud Bava Batra 9a","Genesis 18"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"kindness and charity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"What is the meaning of pikuach nefesh?\nA Repairing the world\nB Saving a life\nC Charity\nD The world to come","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – saving a life. Pikuach nefesh is the principle that saving human life overrides almost all other mitzvot. Repairing the world is tikkun olam, and charity is tzedakah.","evidence":["Leviticus 18:5","Talmud Yoma 85b"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"pikuach nefesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"Which one of the following commandments may a Jew NOT break, even to save their own life?\nA Keeping Shabbat\nB Fasting on Yom Kippur\nC The ban on murder\nD Eating only kosher food","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C – murder. The Talmud (Sanhedrin 74a) teaches that murder, idolatry and forbidden sexual relations may not be committed even to save one's life. Shabbat, fasting and kashrut may all be set aside for pikuach nefesh.","evidence":["Talmud Sanhedrin 74a"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"pikuach nefesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"Give two examples of pikuach nefesh in practice.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Driving to hospital or calling an ambulance on Shabbat.\n• Eating or drinking on Yom Kippur if fasting endangers health.\n• Taking medicine that is not kosher.\n• Doctors working on Shabbat.\n• Delaying brit milah for a sick baby.\n• Donating an organ to save a life.\n• Volunteering with Hatzola on Shabbat.","evidence":["Talmud Yoma 85b"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"pikuach nefesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"Give two Jewish teachings that show that human life is sacred.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Humans are made in God's image (Genesis 1:27).\n• God breathed life into humans (Genesis 2:7).\n• \"Do not murder\" (Exodus 20:13).\n• Saving one life is like saving a whole world (Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5).\n• \"The Lord gave and the Lord has taken away\" (Job 1:21).\n• \"Whoever sheds human blood…\" (Genesis 9:6).","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Genesis 2:7","Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5","Job 1:21"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"sanctity of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"Explain two ways in which the principle of pikuach nefesh influences Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It allows Jews to break Shabbat or festival laws to save life. Normally driving or using a phone is forbidden on Shabbat, but if someone is seriously ill, Jews will call an ambulance or drive to hospital immediately. Volunteers for Hatzola, the Jewish emergency ambulance service in parts of London and Manchester, respond on Shabbat and Yom Kippur because saving life comes first.\n\n2. It shapes decisions about health and medicine. A Jew who is ill, pregnant or diabetic may be told by a rabbi and doctor not to fast on Yom Kippur, or to take medicine containing non-kosher ingredients. Many Jews also support organ donation that saves life, seeing it as a fulfilment of pikuach nefesh.","evidence":["Leviticus 18:5","Talmud Yoma 85b"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"pikuach nefesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"Explain two contrasting Jewish views about abortion.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Orthodox Judaism generally allows abortion only when the mother's life or serious health is at risk. The Mishnah (Oholot 7:6) teaches that if a birth endangers the mother, her life takes priority, because she is a full person and the fetus is not yet. Abortion for social reasons is not usually accepted, because the fetus is potential human life created by God.\n\n2. Reform and Liberal Judaism allow abortion for a wider range of reasons, including the mother's mental health or a serious fetal condition, and leave the decision to the woman's conscience, guided by Jewish values. They stress the woman's wellbeing and her right to make an informed choice, while still treating abortion as a serious moral decision.","evidence":["Mishnah Oholot 7:6","Exodus 21:22–23"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"sanctity of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about the sanctity of human life. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Every human is made in the image of God. Genesis 1:27 says \"God created humanity in his own image\", and God breathed into the first human \"the breath of life\" (Genesis 2:7). This means every life is holy and belongs to God, so murder is forbidden (Exodus 20:13), and many Jews oppose active euthanasia because only God should decide when life ends (\"the Lord gave and the Lord has taken away\", Job 1:21).\n\n2. Each life is of infinite value. The Mishnah (Sanhedrin 4:5) teaches that God created Adam alone to show that \"whoever saves a single life is as if he saved an entire world\". One person is not worth less than many, which is why Jews make great efforts to save even one life, and why pikuach nefesh overrides almost every other law.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Genesis 2:7","Exodus 20:13","Job 1:21","Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"sanctity of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about pikuach nefesh. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Saving life overrides almost every other commandment. Leviticus 18:5 says the laws are those \"by which a person shall live\", and the rabbis explained this as \"live by them, and not die by them\" (Talmud Yoma 85b). So Shabbat, Yom Kippur and the food laws must be set aside if life is in danger; the Talmud even says one should break one Shabbat so the person can keep many future Shabbats.\n\n2. There is a duty to act, not just a permission. \"Do not stand idly by the blood of your neighbour\" (Leviticus 19:16) means a Jew who could save someone must do so. The rabbis said it is praiseworthy to act at once without first asking a rabbi, and even when it is uncertain whether a life is at risk. The only exceptions are murder, idolatry and forbidden sexual relations, which must not be committed even to save a life (Sanhedrin 74a).","evidence":["Leviticus 18:5","Leviticus 19:16","Talmud Yoma 85b","Talmud Sanhedrin 74a"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"pikuach nefesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"\"Saving a life is the most important mitzvah in Judaism.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nPikuach nefesh overrides almost every other commandment. The rabbis read Leviticus 18:5, \"by which a person shall live\", as meaning that the mitzvot must not lead to death (Yoma 85b). So Shabbat, which carries some of the Torah's most serious penalties, and the Yom Kippur fast must both be broken to save a life. If saving a life can override these, it seems to rank above them.\n\nThe Mishnah teaches that saving one life is like saving an entire world (Sanhedrin 4:5), because every person is made in God's image. \"Do not stand idly by the blood of your neighbour\" (Leviticus 19:16) makes it an active duty. Jewish life today reflects this: Hatzola volunteers work on Shabbat, Jewish law supports medicine strongly, and many Jews support organ donation.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nPikuach nefesh does not override everything. A Jew must allow themselves to be killed rather than commit murder, idolatry or forbidden sexual relations (Sanhedrin 74a). This shows that some mitzvot, especially the ban on worshipping other gods, rank even higher, and that faithfulness to God can matter more than one's own life. Jewish history honours martyrs who died rather than renounce God, such as Rabbi Akiva, who died with the Shema on his lips.\n\nOthers argue it is wrong to rank mitzvot at all. Pirkei Avot 2:1 warns that we do not know the reward of each commandment, so we should be equally careful with \"light\" and \"heavy\" ones. Many Jews would also say the most basic mitzvah is to believe in and love the one God (the Shema and the first commandment), because all others flow from it. Some would point to the study of Torah, which the Talmud says is equal to all the others because it leads to doing them.\n\nPikuach nefesh is also an emergency principle: most of the time Jews are not saving lives but keeping the everyday mitzvot that make up their relationship with God.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nSaving a life has an extremely high status in Judaism, overriding nearly every law and showing that the Torah exists to give life. Yet the fact that three prohibitions, above all idolatry, outrank it shows that it is not absolutely the most important. The most convincing conclusion is that pikuach nefesh is the most important principle when a life is in danger, while loyalty to the one God remains the foundation of all the mitzvot.","evidence":["Leviticus 18:5","Leviticus 19:16","Talmud Yoma 85b","Talmud Sanhedrin 74a","Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5","Pirkei Avot 2:1"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"pikuach nefesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","question":"\"Euthanasia can never be acceptable for Jews.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nJudaism teaches that life is sacred because it comes from God. Humans are made in God's image (Genesis 1:27), and \"the Lord gave and the Lord has taken away\" (Job 1:21): only God has the right to end a life. Active euthanasia, deliberately ending a patient's life, is seen as a form of murder, forbidden by the sixth commandment.\n\nEven the last moments of life have value. Jewish law says a goses (a person who is dying) must not be touched in a way that hastens death, and that even a few moments of life are precious, as each life is \"an entire world\" (Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5). Orthodox authorities and most mainstream rabbis oppose legalising assisted dying. Some worry it could put pressure on elderly or disabled people to feel they are a burden.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nMany Jewish authorities distinguish between actively killing and allowing natural death. The 13th-century Sefer Chasidim, quoted by Rabbi Moses Isserles (the Rema) in his notes to the Shulchan Aruch, says one may remove something that is artificially preventing a dying person from dying. On that basis many rabbis allow life-prolonging treatment to be withheld from a terminally ill patient who is suffering, and they allow strong pain relief even if it might shorten life. So not every end-of-life decision is ruled out.\n\nCompassion is also a Jewish value. The Talmud (Ketubot 104a) tells how Rabbi Judah ha-Nasi's maid, seeing his suffering, interrupted the prayers of his students for him to live, and he died. Some see this as permission to pray for death to end suffering. Some Reform and Liberal Jews argue that \"love your neighbour as yourself\" and respect for personal choice could support voluntary euthanasia or assisted dying for the terminally ill, with strict safeguards, and some progressive rabbis in Britain have publicly supported changing the law.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nFor most Jews, and especially in Orthodox teaching, active euthanasia is not acceptable, because it deliberately ends a life that belongs to God. The statement is too absolute, however: Jewish law allows passive measures, such as stopping treatment that only prolongs dying, and some progressive Jews accept assisted dying. The strongest conclusion is that deliberately killing is ruled out by mainstream Judaism, but allowing a natural death with dignity can be acceptable.","evidence":["Genesis 1:27","Job 1:21","Exodus 20:13","Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5","Rema on Shulchan Aruch, Yoreh De'ah 339:1 (citing Sefer Chasidim)","Talmud Ketubot 104a"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"euthanasia"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"How many mitzvot are there in the Torah according to Jewish tradition?\nA 10\nB 248\nC 365\nD 613","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"D – 613. The Talmud (Makkot 23b) counts 613 mitzvot: 248 positive commands and 365 negative commands.","evidence":["Talmud Makkot 23b"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"613 mitzvot"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"What is the yetzer ha-ra?\nA The good inclination\nB The evil or selfish inclination\nC The written law\nD The day of judgement","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – the evil or selfish inclination. Jews believe every person has a yetzer ha-tov (good inclination) and a yetzer ha-ra, and must use free will to master the yetzer ha-ra.","evidence":["Genesis 4:7","Genesis Rabbah 9:7"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"free will"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"Give two Jewish teachings about free will.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• God gives humans the choice to do good or evil (Deuteronomy 30:19).\n• Humans can master sin (Genesis 4:7).\n• \"Everything is foreseen, yet freedom of choice is given\" (Pirkei Avot 3:15).\n• People have a yetzer ha-tov and a yetzer ha-ra.\n• God judges people on their choices.\n• Anyone can repent (teshuvah).","evidence":["Deuteronomy 30:19","Genesis 4:7","Pirkei Avot 3:15"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"free will"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"Give two areas of life covered by the 613 mitzvot.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Worship and prayer.\n• Shabbat and festivals.\n• Food (kashrut).\n• Family life and marriage.\n• Charity and treatment of the poor.\n• Business and honesty.\n• Agriculture.\n• The Temple and sacrifices.","evidence":["Leviticus 11","Leviticus 19","Exodus 20"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"613 mitzvot"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"Explain two contrasting Jewish beliefs about the 613 mitzvot.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Orthodox Jews believe that all the mitzvot were given by God at Sinai and are binding for ever. Although humans have free will to obey or disobey, they cannot choose which commands to accept. So Orthodox Jews try to keep every mitzvah that can be kept today, such as all the laws of Shabbat and kashrut, as interpreted by halakhah.\n\n2. Reform Jews believe the mitzvot were inspired by God but recorded by humans in a particular time. They use their free will and conscience to make informed choices about ritual mitzvot. For example, a Reform Jew might light Shabbat candles but drive to synagogue, or not keep a fully kosher kitchen, while treating ethical mitzvot such as justice and charity as essential.","evidence":["Exodus 24:7","Talmud Makkot 23b"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"613 mitzvot: diversity"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in free will influences Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Jews take responsibility for their actions. Because God gives people the choice between good and evil (\"choose life\", Deuteronomy 30:19), Jews cannot blame God or fate for their wrong actions. This encourages them to think carefully about their choices, such as being honest in business or kind to others.\n\n2. It encourages repentance and self-improvement. Since people are free, they can change. At Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur Jews examine their behaviour, apologise to people they have wronged, and resolve to do better (teshuvah). Many also try to strengthen their yetzer ha-tov by taking on extra mitzvot, such as daily study or prayer.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 30:19","Maimonides, Laws of Repentance 5"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"free will"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about free will. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. God gives every person the choice between good and evil. In Deuteronomy 30:19 God says, \"I have set before you life and death, blessing and curse; therefore choose life.\" Even Cain was told that sin \"crouches at the door… but you can master it\" (Genesis 4:7). Humans are therefore morally responsible for what they choose, and God judges them on those choices.\n\n2. Free will exists alongside God's knowledge. Rabbi Akiva taught that \"everything is foreseen, yet freedom of choice is given\" (Pirkei Avot 3:15). God knows what will happen, but this does not force anyone's choices. The rabbis add that each person has a yetzer ha-tov and a yetzer ha-ra, and that keeping the mitzvot helps them follow the good inclination. As the Talmud says, \"everything is in the hands of heaven except the fear of heaven\" (Berakhot 33b).","evidence":["Deuteronomy 30:19","Genesis 4:7","Pirkei Avot 3:15","Talmud Berakhot 33b"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"free will"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"Explain two reasons why the 613 mitzvot are important to Jews. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. They are the terms of the covenant with God. At Sinai the people promised, \"All that the Lord has spoken we will do, and we will understand\" (Exodus 24:7). Keeping the mitzvot is how Jews fulfil their side of the covenant and show love for God; \"you shall love the Lord your God… and these words which I command you shall be upon your heart\" (Deuteronomy 6:5–6).\n\n2. They make the whole of life holy. The Talmud (Makkot 23b) links the 248 positive commands to the parts of the body and the 365 negative commands to the days of the year, teaching that every part of a person and every day can serve God. Through blessings over food, kashrut, Shabbat and ethical laws, ordinary activities become acts of worship. The mitzvot also help people use their free will well, strengthening the good inclination.","evidence":["Exodus 24:7","Deuteronomy 6:5–6","Talmud Makkot 23b"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"613 mitzvot"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"\"If God has given humans free will, Jews should be free to choose which mitzvot to keep.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nReform Judaism argues that free will and conscience are gifts from God that should be used, not ignored. Reform Jews believe the Torah was inspired by God but written by humans, and that revelation continues. Each Jew should therefore study the tradition and make an \"informed choice\" about which ritual mitzvot are meaningful today. This allows Judaism to adapt, for example by giving women equal roles in worship.\n\nSome mitzvot cannot be kept today anyway, such as the Temple sacrifices, and the rabbis themselves adapted the law over time. Jewish life in Britain is diverse, and many Jews keep some mitzvot, such as Pesach and Yom Kippur, for family and cultural reasons. Forcing people to keep everything might push them away entirely, whereas choice can lead to more sincere observance.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOrthodox Jews argue that the statement misunderstands free will. Free will means we are able to obey or disobey God (Deuteronomy 30:19, \"choose life\"), not that we have the right to decide which commands are valid. A commandment that you can opt out of is no longer a commandment. At Sinai the people accepted the whole Torah, saying \"we will do and we will understand\" (Exodus 24:7), and Jews believe that this covenant binds every generation.\n\nPirkei Avot 2:1 teaches that one should be as careful with a minor mitzvah as with a major one, because no one knows their true reward. Even mitzvot that seem to have no clear reason (chukim), such as the food laws, are kept as acts of obedience and love. If each person chooses, Orthodox Jews argue, Judaism risks becoming a matter of personal taste and could lose its distinct identity.\n\nA middle view, held by many Masorti and traditional Jews, is that the mitzvot are binding but halakhah can develop through rabbinic interpretation rather than individual choice.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe statement reflects the Reform view and recognises that Jews in practice do make choices. However, it is weaker as an argument, because having free will does not logically mean that every choice is right; Judaism teaches that free will exists precisely so that people can choose to obey God. Orthodox Judaism's position is more consistent with the idea of a commandment, while Reform Judaism makes a strong case for conscience in interpreting ritual law. Overall the statement is not convincing for the ethical mitzvot, which all Jews regard as binding, but remains a real debate for ritual ones.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 30:19","Exodus 24:7","Pirkei Avot 2:1","Reform principle of informed choice"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"free will and the mitzvot"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","question":"\"Humans cannot really have free will if God knows everything.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nIf God is all-knowing, as Jews believe, then God already knows every decision a person will make. Some argue that if God knows I will do something, I cannot do otherwise, so my choice is not really free. The Torah also contains examples where God seems to control people's choices, for example God \"hardening Pharaoh's heart\" (Exodus 9:12), which suggests that people are not always free.\n\nSome people also argue from science or psychology that our choices are shaped by genes, upbringing and environment, so even without God free will may be an illusion. If that is true, Jewish teachings about reward, punishment and repentance would be hard to justify.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nJudaism firmly teaches that humans do have free will. Rabbi Akiva said, \"Everything is foreseen, yet freedom of choice is given\" (Pirkei Avot 3:15). Both are true, even if humans cannot fully understand how. Maimonides argued that God's knowledge is not like human knowledge: it is part of God's own nature, so it does not cause our actions. Knowing that something will happen is not the same as making it happen.\n\nThe whole Torah assumes free will. God says, \"I have set before you life and death… choose life\" (Deuteronomy 30:19), and tells Cain that he can master sin (Genesis 4:7). Commandments, judgement and teshuvah would make no sense if humans could not choose. Maimonides wrote that free will is \"a great principle and a pillar of the Torah\". Many commentators explain Pharaoh's hardened heart as the result of his own repeated choices; for the first plagues the text says Pharaoh hardened his own heart.\n\nThe Talmud teaches that \"everything is in the hands of heaven except the fear of heaven\" (Berakhot 33b): God may decide many things about a person's life, but whether they obey God remains their choice.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe argument that God's knowledge removes free will is a serious philosophical puzzle, but it is not convincing from a Jewish point of view. Judaism holds that foreknowledge does not mean control, and the whole system of mitzvot, judgement and repentance depends on real choice. Even if the puzzle cannot be fully solved, Rabbi Akiva's teaching shows that Jews have long accepted both truths together. So the statement should be rejected.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 3:15","Deuteronomy 30:19","Genesis 4:7","Exodus 8:15, 9:12","Maimonides, Laws of Repentance 5:3–5","Talmud Berakhot 33b"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"free will"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"Which one of the following is a mitzvah between a person and God?\nA Giving to charity\nB Visiting the sick\nC Keeping Shabbat\nD Paying workers on time","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C – keeping Shabbat is a mitzvah bein adam la-Makom (between a person and God), linked to remembering God's creation. The other three concern how people treat each other.","evidence":["Exodus 20:8–11"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"mitzvot between man and God"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"Which one of the following is a mitzvah between people?\nA Fixing a mezuzah\nB Wearing tefillin\nC Not gossiping (lashon ha-ra)\nD Saying the Shema","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C – not gossiping. Lashon ha-ra (harmful speech) damages other people, so the prohibition is a mitzvah bein adam la-chavero. The other three are ritual mitzvot directed towards God.","evidence":["Leviticus 19:16"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"mitzvot between people"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"Give two examples of mitzvot between a person and God.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Keeping Shabbat.\n• Praying.\n• Saying the Shema.\n• Keeping kosher.\n• Wearing tefillin or tzitzit.\n• Fixing a mezuzah.\n• Not making idols.\n• Not misusing God's name.\n• Fasting on Yom Kippur.\n• Studying Torah.","evidence":["Exodus 20:2–11","Deuteronomy 6:4–9"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"mitzvot between man and God"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"Give two examples of mitzvot between a person and other people.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Honour your parents.\n• Do not murder.\n• Do not steal.\n• Do not lie or bear false witness.\n• Give tzedakah.\n• Love your neighbour.\n• Visit the sick.\n• Do not gossip.\n• Pay workers on time.\n• Use honest weights.","evidence":["Exodus 20:12–17","Leviticus 19:13–18"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"mitzvot between people"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"Explain two differences between mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. They are directed at different relationships. Mitzvot between a person and God, such as prayer, keeping Shabbat and kashrut, express worship and holiness. Mitzvot between people, such as giving tzedakah, honesty and not gossiping, concern justice and kindness in society. The two tablets of the Ten Commandments are traditionally divided in this way.\n\n2. They are atoned for differently. The Mishnah (Yoma 8:9) teaches that Yom Kippur atones for sins against God, but sins against another person are not forgiven until the wronged person has been reconciled. So Jews must apologise, and repay any loss, to people they have hurt before Yom Kippur, whereas sins against God can be repented directly through prayer and fasting.","evidence":["Mishnah Yoma 8:9","Exodus 20:2–17"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"difference between the two"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"Explain two ways in which mitzvot between people influence Jews today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. They shape Jewish attitudes to speech. Jews are taught to avoid lashon ha-ra, harmful gossip, even if it is true, because it damages people's reputations. Some Jews study the laws of speech daily, following the teaching of the Chafetz Chaim, and try to speak positively about others.\n\n2. They shape preparation for the High Holy Days. Because Yom Kippur does not atone for wrongs against other people until they have been put right, many Jews contact family, friends and colleagues in the days before Yom Kippur to apologise and ask forgiveness. This makes repairing relationships part of religious life.","evidence":["Leviticus 19:16","Mishnah Yoma 8:9"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"mitzvot between people today"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"Explain two Jewish teachings about the importance of mitzvot between people. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Loving one's neighbour is a central principle of the Torah. Leviticus 19:18 commands, \"Love your neighbour as yourself\", and Rabbi Akiva called this a great principle of the Torah. Hillel summed up the whole Torah as \"What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour\" (Shabbat 31a). So caring for others is not an optional extra but the heart of Jewish life.\n\n2. Wrongs against people need to be put right with people, not only with God. The Mishnah (Yoma 8:9) teaches that Yom Kippur atones for sins against God, but not for sins against others until the person wronged has been reconciled. This shows that God cares about human relationships and that Jews cannot use religious ritual to avoid responsibility for hurting others.","evidence":["Leviticus 19:18","Talmud Shabbat 31a","Mishnah Yoma 8:9"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"mitzvot between people"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"Explain two ways in which mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man are linked. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Both come from the same God and appear side by side in the Torah. The Ten Commandments contain both kinds on two tablets, and Leviticus 19 mixes keeping Shabbat and avoiding idols with leaving food for the poor and loving one's neighbour, all under the command \"Be holy, for I the Lord your God am holy\" (Leviticus 19:2). This shows they are equally part of the covenant.\n\n2. Serving people is a way of serving God. Because every person is made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), how we treat people reflects how we honour God: \"whoever mocks the poor insults his Maker\" (Proverbs 17:5). The prophets taught that worship without justice is unacceptable to God (Amos 5:21–24), so the two kinds of mitzvot cannot be separated.","evidence":["Leviticus 19:2","Genesis 1:27","Proverbs 17:5","Amos 5:21–24"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"links between the two"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"\"Mitzvot between people are more important than mitzvot between people and God.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Mishnah teaches that Yom Kippur atones for sins against God, but not for sins against other people until those people have been reconciled (Yoma 8:9). This suggests that wronging a person is in some ways more serious, because God cannot simply forgive it on the victim's behalf.\n\nThe prophets repeatedly put ethics above ritual. Amos records God saying, \"I hate, I despise your festivals… but let justice roll down like waters\" (Amos 5:21–24), and Isaiah says God will not listen to prayers from people whose \"hands are full of blood\" (Isaiah 1:15). Hillel summed up the whole Torah as \"What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour\". Many Reform Jews would agree that the ethical mitzvot are the lasting core of Judaism, while ritual practices are ways to support them. Pikuach nefesh, saving a human life, overrides almost every ritual law, which again suggests people come first.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOrthodox Jews would argue that all mitzvot come from God and that it is wrong to rank them; Pirkei Avot 2:1 warns that we do not know the reward of each commandment. The first of the Ten Commandments is about God (\"I am the Lord your God\"), and the Shema, \"Hear O Israel… the Lord is One\", is the foundation of everything else. The three laws that may not be broken even to save a life include idolatry, which is a mitzvah between a person and God.\n\nThe two kinds of mitzvot are also linked. People should be treated well because they are made in God's image, so ethics rests on a relationship with God. Mitzvot such as Shabbat, prayer and Torah study shape a person's character and keep the ethical commands alive in their life. Without the relationship with God, the moral commands lose their authority. The prophets attacked worship without justice, not worship itself.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThere is real Jewish support for the statement, especially in Mishnah Yoma 8:9 and the prophets, and in Reform emphasis on ethics. However, Judaism as a whole does not set the two against each other: the same Torah commands both, and loving one's neighbour flows from loving God. The most convincing view is that neither is more important, but that ritual devotion without ethical behaviour is empty, so Jews cannot claim to serve God while mistreating people.","evidence":["Mishnah Yoma 8:9","Amos 5:21–24","Isaiah 1:15–17","Pirkei Avot 2:1","Talmud Shabbat 31a","Exodus 20:2"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"mitzvot between people and God"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","question":"\"It is impossible to keep all the mitzvot in the modern world.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nMany of the 613 mitzvot literally cannot be kept today. Laws about Temple sacrifices, the priesthood and many agricultural laws depend on the Temple in Jerusalem or the land of Israel, and the Temple has not stood since 70 CE. Even the strictest Orthodox Jew cannot keep all 613; Jewish tradition itself recognises this.\n\nModern life also makes some mitzvot very demanding. Keeping Shabbat may clash with work or school timetables, which often include Saturdays, keeping strictly kosher can be expensive and hard when eating out, and praying three times a day fits awkwardly into a working day. Many Jews in Britain, including many Reform and secular Jews, keep only some mitzvot, which suggests that full observance feels unrealistic for many.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nOrthodox Jews would say that it is possible to keep all the mitzvot that apply today, and thousands of British Jews do so. Communities in places such as Golders Green, Stamford Hill, Gateshead and Manchester have kosher shops, eruvs, synagogues and schools that make full observance practical. UK equality law also protects religious practice, and many employers allow early finishes on Friday in winter for Shabbat.\n\nHalakhah has always adapted to new situations without abandoning the law: rabbis rule on electricity, timers, medical technology and online life. The Torah says the commandments are \"not too hard for you… it is very near you, in your mouth and in your heart, so that you can do it\" (Deuteronomy 30:11–14). God would not command what humans cannot do. The rabbis also taught that one who studies the laws of the sacrifices is considered as if they had offered them, so even Temple-related mitzvot are honoured through study.\n\nReform Jews might reply that the question is not whether it is possible but whether it is required, since they believe in informed choice about ritual mitzvot. On that view, modern life is a reason to reinterpret rather than abandon the mitzvot.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nThe statement is true in a narrow, literal sense: no one can keep all 613 today because many depend on the Temple. But it is not true that the mitzvot which apply today are impossible, as thriving Orthodox communities show. Modern life makes observance harder, not impossible. The stronger conclusion is that the commandments that can be kept are still being kept, and Deuteronomy's teaching that they are \"not too hard\" remains convincing for observant Jews.","evidence":["Deuteronomy 30:11–14","Talmud Menachot 110a","Talmud Makkot 23b"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"613 mitzvot today"}],"paper2":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"Which Hebrew name for the synagogue means 'house of assembly'?\nA Beit Tefilah\nB Beit Midrash\nC Beit Knesset\nD Beit Din","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C – Beit Knesset, the house of assembly or meeting. Beit Tefilah is the house of prayer, Beit Midrash the house of study, and Beit Din is a rabbinical court.","evidence":["Hebrew names of the synagogue"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"synagogue"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"What is a minyan?\nA A prayer shawl\nB The minimum of ten adults needed for certain communal prayers\nC The reading desk in the synagogue\nD The leader of the synagogue","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B. A minyan is the quorum of ten adults needed for certain prayers, such as the Kaddish and the public reading of the Torah. Orthodox synagogues count men only, while Reform and Liberal synagogues count women and men.","evidence":["Talmud Berakhot 21b","Pirkei Avot 3:6"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"minyan"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"Give two purposes of the synagogue.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• House of prayer (Beit Tefilah).\n• House of study (Beit Midrash).\n• House of assembly or community centre (Beit Knesset).\n• Keeping the Torah scrolls.\n• Celebrating life events such as Bar Mitzvah and weddings.\n• A base for charity work.\n• Education of children (cheder).","evidence":["Beit Tefilah, Beit Midrash, Beit Knesset"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"synagogue"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"Give two activities, other than services, that may take place in a synagogue.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Hebrew or cheder classes for children.\n• Adult Torah study.\n• Youth clubs.\n• Kiddush and social events.\n• Bar or Bat Mitzvah preparation.\n• Charity collections or food bank.\n• Support groups for the elderly or bereaved.\n• Wedding receptions or celebrations.\n• Interfaith events.","evidence":["Beit Knesset and Beit Midrash roles"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"synagogue"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"Explain two reasons why the synagogue is important to the Jewish community.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It is the place of communal worship. Some important prayers, such as the Kaddish and the reading of the Torah, can only be said with a minyan of ten. The synagogue brings people together for Shabbat and festival services, and the rabbis taught that God's presence rests among ten people who pray together.\n\n2. It holds the community together. As the Beit Knesset, the house of assembly, it offers education for children, adult study, youth clubs and support for the sick, elderly and bereaved. In Britain, where Jews are a small minority, it helps people keep their Jewish identity, celebrate life-cycle events together and pass on traditions to the next generation.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 3:6","Talmud Berakhot 6a"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"importance of synagogue"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"Explain two contrasting ways in which Orthodox and Reform Jews use the synagogue.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. In Orthodox synagogues, services are almost entirely in Hebrew, men and women sit separately (often with women in a gallery or behind a mechitzah), and only men count towards the minyan and lead the services. Many Orthodox synagogues hold daily morning and evening services, and members walk to the synagogue on Shabbat.\n\n2. In Reform synagogues, men and women sit together and participate equally: women can be rabbis, read from the Torah and count in the minyan. Services often include much more English, and some use musical instruments such as an organ. Many Reform synagogues hold services mainly on Shabbat and festivals rather than daily, and members may drive to services.","evidence":["Mechitzah","Reform egalitarian practice"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Orthodox and Reform synagogues"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"Explain two reasons why prayer in the synagogue is important for Jews. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Synagogue prayer replaced the Temple sacrifices. After the Temple was destroyed in 70 CE, the rabbis taught that prayer is \"the service of the heart\" (Ta'anit 2a), fulfilling Hosea's words, \"we will offer the words of our lips instead of bulls\" (Hosea 14:3). The three daily services match the times of the Temple offerings, so synagogue worship continues the worship of the Temple.\n\n2. Praying as a community brings special holiness. Some prayers, such as Kaddish and the Kedushah, need a minyan of ten. The rabbis taught that when ten gather, the Shekhinah (God's presence) is among them (Pirkei Avot 3:6). Praying together also expresses that Jews stand before God as one people, which is why most prayers are written in the plural (\"our God\", \"forgive us\").","evidence":["Hosea 14:3","Talmud Ta'anit 2a","Pirkei Avot 3:6"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"synagogue prayer"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"Explain two ways in which the synagogue is a place of study. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Torah study is a central mitzvah, and the synagogue is a Beit Midrash, a house of study. The Mishnah lists the study of Torah as equal to all the other commandments, because it leads to all of them (Mishnah Peah 1:1). Synagogues run shiurim (classes) on the weekly Torah portion, Talmud and Jewish law, and many members join study partnerships (chavruta).\n\n2. It educates children and passes on the tradition. The Shema commands, \"teach them diligently to your children\" (Deuteronomy 6:7). Synagogues run cheder classes where children learn Hebrew, prayers, festivals and Jewish history, and they prepare young people for Bar and Bat Mitzvah, when they read from the Torah in front of the congregation.","evidence":["Mishnah Peah 1:1","Deuteronomy 6:7"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"synagogue as place of study"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"\"Jews do not need to go to the synagogue to be religious.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nMuch of Jewish practice takes place at home rather than in the synagogue. Shabbat begins at home with candle-lighting, Kiddush and challah; the Pesach seder is a home ritual; kashrut is kept in the kitchen; and the mezuzah is on the doorpost of the home. The home is often called a mikdash me'at, a small sanctuary, so a Jew can live a deeply religious life centred on the family.\n\nPrayer does not need a building. Jews can pray the Shema and the Amidah privately, anywhere, and God listens to every sincere prayer. The Tenakh shows people praying alone: Hannah (1 Samuel 1) and Daniel (Daniel 6:11). Judaism also values ethical action, such as tzedakah, kindness and justice, which happens in daily life rather than in a building. For many Reform and secular Jews, Jewish identity is expressed through family, culture and social action rather than regular attendance.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nSome important mitzvot can only be fulfilled with a community. The Torah reading, the Kaddish (for example, a mourner saying Kaddish for a parent) and the Kedushah all need a minyan, which a person cannot form alone. Orthodox men are expected to pray with a minyan where possible, three times a day, and the rabbis taught that God's presence rests among ten who pray together.\n\nThe synagogue also protects Jewish life for the future. It is a Beit Midrash where children and adults study Torah, and a Beit Knesset that supports the sick, elderly and bereaved. In Britain, where Jews are a small minority, it provides community and identity that individuals might not be able to sustain alone. Rabbi Hillel taught, \"Do not separate yourself from the community\" (Pirkei Avot 2:4), which suggests that being religious is not only a private matter.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nIt is true that a Jew can be religious without attending the synagogue regularly, because so much of Judaism is lived at home and in everyday ethics. However, the synagogue is needed for communal prayer, the Torah reading and Kaddish, and Jewish teaching discourages separating oneself from the community. The fairest conclusion is that the synagogue is not the only place to be religious, but a fully observant Jewish life, especially in Orthodox understanding, needs it.","evidence":["Pirkei Avot 2:4","Pirkei Avot 3:6","1 Samuel 1:10–13","Daniel 6:11"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"importance of synagogue"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"The synagogue and its importance","question":"\"The most important purpose of the synagogue is as a place of prayer.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe synagogue is above all a Beit Tefilah, a house of prayer. After the Temple was destroyed, prayer replaced sacrifice: \"we will offer the words of our lips instead of bulls\" (Hosea 14:3). The three daily services follow the pattern of the Temple offerings, and the synagogue is called a mikdash me'at, a small sanctuary. Its most important features, the Aron Hakodesh facing Jerusalem, the ner tamid and the bimah, are all designed for worship.\n\nCommunal prayer requires a synagogue and a minyan. The Torah is read publicly on Shabbat, Mondays and Thursdays, mourners say Kaddish, and the High Holy Days and festivals are celebrated together in services. The rabbis taught that the Shekhinah rests on ten people praying together, so it is through prayer that the synagogue becomes holy.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nSome argue study is more important. The synagogue is also a Beit Midrash, and the Mishnah says that the study of Torah is equal to all other commandments (Peah 1:1). The Talmud even says that a synagogue may be turned into a house of study, which has greater holiness, but not the other way round (Megillah 27a). This suggests that, in rabbinic thinking, study ranks above prayer.\n\nOthers say the community role matters most today. As a Beit Knesset, the synagogue holds Jewish identity together: education for children, youth groups, support for the elderly, food banks and life-cycle celebrations. Many British Jews who rarely pray regularly still belong to a synagogue for these reasons, and for Reform and Liberal Jews community and social action (tikkun olam) are central. Without the community, there would be no minyan anyway.\n\nIt can also be argued that the three purposes cannot be separated: Jews study in order to pray and act, and they pray as a community.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nPrayer is the most visible purpose of the synagogue, and its design centres on worship. However, rabbinic teaching ranks study highly, and for many British Jews the community role is what keeps them connected. The most convincing conclusion is that prayer, study and community are three sides of one purpose, bringing Jews closer to God and to each other, so prayer is central but not clearly the most important on its own.","evidence":["Hosea 14:3","Mishnah Peah 1:1","Talmud Megillah 27a","Pirkei Avot 3:6"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"purposes of synagogue"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"What is the ner tamid?\nA The reading platform\nB The ever-burning light\nC The curtain in front of the ark\nD The pointer used to read the Torah","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B – the ever-burning light, which hangs above or near the ark. It recalls the Temple menorah that burned continually (Exodus 27:20) and symbolises God's eternal presence. The reading platform is the bimah, the curtain is the parochet, and the pointer is the yad.","evidence":["Exodus 27:20–21"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"ner tamid"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"Which feature of a synagogue holds the Torah scrolls?\nA Bimah\nB Mechitzah\nC Aron Hakodesh\nD Ner tamid","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C – the Aron Hakodesh, the holy ark, which holds the Sefer Torah scrolls. It is placed on the wall facing Jerusalem and recalls the Ark of the Covenant in the Temple.","evidence":["Exodus 25:10–22 (Ark of the Covenant)"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Write only the letter of the answer you choose; one mark is for the correct term or fact.","answerTopic":"Aron Hakodesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"Give two features found in most synagogues.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Aron Hakodesh (ark).\n• Sefer Torah scrolls.\n• Ner tamid.\n• Bimah.\n• Parochet (curtain).\n• Ten Commandments tablets.\n• Menorah.\n• Seats facing Jerusalem.\n• Women's gallery or mechitzah (Orthodox).","evidence":["Exodus 27:20","Nehemiah 8:4"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"synagogue features"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"Give two ways in which Jews show respect for the Torah scrolls in the synagogue.","marks":2,"command":"Give two","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two from:\n• Keeping them in the ark behind a curtain.\n• Standing when the ark is opened.\n• Using a yad instead of touching the text.\n• Dressing them in a mantle and crown.\n• Kissing them, or touching them with a tallit, as they are carried round.\n• Lifting them for all to see (hagbah).\n• Reading them from a raised bimah.\n• Burying worn-out scrolls.","evidence":["Hagbah","Yad"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points; short phrases are enough. Do not spend time explaining, and do not repeat the same idea in different words.","answerTopic":"Torah scrolls"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"Explain two differences between Orthodox and Reform synagogues.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Seating. In Orthodox synagogues men and women sit separately, either with women in an upstairs gallery or behind a mechitzah (screen), so that people can concentrate on prayer without distraction. In Reform synagogues men and women sit together as families, reflecting the Reform belief in equality in worship.\n\n2. The position of the bimah. In Orthodox synagogues the bimah is traditionally in the centre, with seats around it, showing that the Torah is at the heart of the community. In most Reform synagogues the bimah is at the front, next to the ark, and the leader faces the congregation, more like a stage. Reform synagogues may also have an organ, which Orthodox synagogues do not use on Shabbat.","evidence":["Mechitzah","Talmud Sukkah 51b (separation in the Temple courts)"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"Orthodox and Reform synagogues"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"Explain two ways in which features of the synagogue remind Jews of the Temple in Jerusalem.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The Aron Hakodesh recalls the Ark of the Covenant. In the Temple, the Ark held the tablets of the Ten Commandments in the Holy of Holies. The synagogue ark holds the Torah scrolls, is covered with a curtain (parochet) like the one in the Tabernacle, and is the holiest part of the building, so people stand when it is opened.\n\n2. The ner tamid recalls the Temple menorah. God commanded that a lamp should burn \"continually\" in the Tabernacle (Exodus 27:20). The synagogue's ever-burning light reminds Jews of the Temple and of God's constant presence. Synagogues also face Jerusalem, where the Temple stood, so prayers are directed towards the place of the ancient sanctuary.","evidence":["Exodus 25:10–22","Exodus 26:31–33","Exodus 27:20"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points. Develop each one (a simple point earns 1 mark, a detailed one 2 marks) by saying how or why it shapes what Jews believe or do today. If the question asks for contrasting ways, make sure the two are genuinely different, for example Orthodox and Reform.","answerTopic":"synagogue and Temple"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"Explain two reasons why the Aron Hakodesh is important in a synagogue. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It holds the Sefer Torah, the most sacred object in Judaism. The Torah scrolls contain God's teaching given to Moses at Sinai. Because of this, the ark is the focal point of the synagogue, worshippers face it when praying, and they stand when it is opened, showing respect for God's word.\n\n2. It recalls the Ark of the Covenant in the Temple. God commanded Moses to make an ark to hold the tablets of the covenant and said, \"There I will meet with you\" (Exodus 25:22). The synagogue ark is placed on the wall facing Jerusalem and covered by a parochet, like the curtain in the Tabernacle (Exodus 26:31–33). It connects Jews today to the Temple and to God's presence.","evidence":["Exodus 25:10–22","Exodus 26:31–33"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"Aron Hakodesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"Explain two reasons why the bimah and the ner tamid are important. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The bimah shows the importance of reading the Torah publicly. When Ezra read the Torah to the people, he stood \"on a wooden platform made for the purpose\" so all could hear (Nehemiah 8:4). The raised bimah honours the Torah and allows the whole congregation to hear the reading on Shabbat, Mondays, Thursdays and festivals. In Orthodox synagogues its central position shows the Torah at the heart of the community.\n\n2. The ner tamid symbolises God's eternal presence. God commanded that the lamp in the Tabernacle should burn \"continually\" (Exodus 27:20). The ever-burning light reminds Jews that God is always present and that the light of Torah never goes out, and it links each synagogue to the Temple in Jerusalem.","evidence":["Nehemiah 8:4","Exodus 27:20–21"],"examTip":"Two developed beliefs or teachings (up to 2 marks each) plus 1 mark for a relevant reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish teaching, such as the Torah, Tenakh, Talmud or Mishnah. Name the source clearly; it does not have to be word-for-word.","answerTopic":"bimah and ner tamid"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"\"The Aron Hakodesh is the most important feature of a synagogue.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nThe Aron Hakodesh holds the Sefer Torah, the holiest object in Judaism, containing God's words given at Sinai. It is placed on the wall facing Jerusalem, and it is the focal point towards which the whole congregation faces when praying. People stand when it is opened, and opening it is an honour, which shows its special status.\n\nIt links the synagogue to the Temple. The ark recalls the Ark of the Covenant, which held the tablets of the Ten Commandments in the Holy of Holies, where God said \"there I will meet with you\" (Exodus 25:22). The parochet that covers it recalls the Tabernacle curtain. Without the Torah scrolls in the ark, many would say that a building is just a hall, not a true synagogue.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nIt could be argued that the Sefer Torah itself, not the cupboard that holds it, is what matters. The ark is important only because of the scroll inside. Equally, the bimah, where the Torah is actually read to the people, could be seen as more important, because hearing God's word is the purpose of having the scroll. In Orthodox synagogues the bimah is at the centre, showing the Torah at the heart of the community.\n\nThe ner tamid is also deeply significant, as it symbolises God's eternal presence and recalls the Temple menorah. Some would argue that the most important \"feature\" is not a piece of furniture at all but the minyan, the community of ten who pray together, since the rabbis taught that the Shekhinah rests on them (Pirkei Avot 3:6). Jews prayed together for centuries in simple rooms and in hiding, which shows that the community is more essential than the furniture.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nOf the physical features, the Aron Hakodesh has the strongest claim to be the most important, because it holds the Torah and gives the synagogue its focus and direction. However, its importance comes from the Torah inside it, and a synagogue truly comes alive only when a community gathers to pray and read the Torah. So the statement is true of the building's features, but the people and the Torah matter more than any feature.","evidence":["Exodus 25:22","Exodus 26:31–33","Pirkei Avot 3:6"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Aron Hakodesh"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"The design and religious features of synagogues","question":"\"Orthodox and Reform synagogues are more alike than different.\"\n\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n• refer to Jewish teaching\n• give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n• give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n• reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\n\nBoth Orthodox and Reform synagogues have the same essential features. Each has an Aron Hakodesh on the wall facing Jerusalem containing handwritten Torah scrolls, a ner tamid recalling the Temple menorah, and a bimah from which the Torah is read. Neither has images of God or people, because of the commandment against images (Exodus 20:4).\n\nTheir purposes are also the same. Both are a Beit Tefilah, Beit Midrash and Beit Knesset: a house of prayer, study and community. Both read the weekly Torah portion on Shabbat, celebrate the same festivals, hold Bar and Bat Mitzvah ceremonies and support their members through life-cycle events. Both teach the Shema and the core prayers such as the Amidah, and both teach Jewish ethics. A Jew entering either would recognise it as a synagogue.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\n\nThere are significant differences in layout and worship. Orthodox synagogues separate men and women with a mechitzah or gallery, whereas Reform synagogues have mixed seating. The Orthodox bimah is often in the centre, while the Reform bimah is usually at the front with the ark. Reform synagogues may use an organ or instruments on Shabbat, which Orthodox synagogues do not.\n\nThese differences reflect deeper beliefs. Orthodox Judaism holds that the Torah and halakhah are God-given and unchanging, so traditional practices are kept. Reform Judaism believes in progressive revelation and equality, so women can be rabbis, read from the Torah and count in the minyan. Services in Reform synagogues use more English and are often shorter, and some prayers, for example those for restoring sacrifices, are changed or removed. For some Jews, these differences are so important that they would not pray in the other kind of synagogue.\n\nJUSTIFIED CONCLUSION\n\nBoth views have strength. The shared features and purposes show a common Jewish heritage centred on the Torah. The differences are not only about furniture, however; they reflect different understandings of revelation, law and gender roles. The most convincing conclusion is that they are alike in their essential features and purposes, but different in ways that matter deeply to the worshippers, so the statement is broadly true of the building but less true of the worship.","evidence":["Exodus 20:4","Mechitzah","Reform principle of progressive revelation"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with Jewish teaching, explain why each argument matters (do not just list views), weigh them, and finish with a conclusion that follows from what you have written. Include Orthodox and Reform views where they differ. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are also assessed on 12-mark answers.","answerTopic":"Orthodox and Reform synagogues"}]},"notes":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","title":"The nature of God: God as one, God as Creator, God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","specFocus":"Key beliefs: the nature of God – God as one (the Shema), God as Creator, and God as Law-Giver and Judge who is also loving and merciful; how these beliefs shape Jewish life today.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"The nature of God\nJudaism is strictly monotheistic: there is one God, who has no partner, form or body and cannot be divided. Everything else in Jewish belief and practice rests on this.\n\nGod as one\n• The Shema (Deuteronomy 6:4): \"Hear, O Israel: the Lord is our God, the Lord is One.\" It is said morning and evening, written inside the mezuzah and tefillin, and traditionally said as the last words before death.\n• God's oneness means God is unique and indivisible. Jews therefore reject any idea of God taking human form, or of more than one divine person.\n• The first two of the Ten Commandments forbid worshipping other gods and making idols (Exodus 20:2–5). For this reason synagogues contain no images of God.\n• Maimonides' Thirteen Principles of Faith (12th century) list God's unity as the second principle. Many Orthodox Jews sing them in the hymn Yigdal.\n\nGod as Creator\n• \"In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth\" (Genesis 1:1). God created everything, and created it good (Genesis 1:31).\n• Humans are made \"in the image of God\" (b'tselem Elohim, Genesis 1:27), which gives every life dignity and responsibility.\n• Shabbat recalls God resting on the seventh day (Genesis 2:2–3), so creation is remembered every week.\n• God is separate from the universe (transcendent) yet still involved in it. Orthodox Jews often read Genesis as God's revealed account. Many Reform and Liberal Jews, and many Orthodox Jews too, accept the Big Bang and evolution as the way God's creation unfolded.\n\nGod as Law-Giver\n• God gave the Torah to Moses at Sinai, including the Ten Commandments and, by tradition, 613 mitzvot (commandments). Obeying the law is a way of serving God.\n• Orthodox Jews believe both the written and the oral law come from God and are binding. Reform Jews see the law as divinely inspired but written down by humans, so they adapt it with informed personal choice.\n\nGod as Judge, loving and merciful\n• God judges people's actions, both in this life and after death. At Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur Jews pray to be written in the \"Book of Life\".\n• God's judgement is balanced by mercy: \"The Lord, the Lord, a God compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in kindness and faithfulness\" (Exodus 34:6). These \"Thirteen Attributes of Mercy\" are recited on festivals and fast days.\n• The rabbis taught that God created the world using both the attribute of justice (din) and the attribute of mercy (rachamim), because a world run on strict justice alone could not survive.\n• Because God is loving, Jews believe God forgives those who do teshuvah (repentance, literally \"returning\").\n\nInfluence today\nThese beliefs shape daily prayer (the Shema and Amidah), the blessings said before food, Shabbat, the High Holy Days and ethical behaviour, since all humans bear God's image. Many Jews avoid writing the divine name in full and write \"G-d\" instead.\n\nKey terms: Shema; monotheism; b'tselem Elohim; Torah; mitzvot; din (justice); rachamim (mercy); teshuvah."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","title":"The divine presence (Shekhinah)","specFocus":"Key beliefs: the divine presence (Shekhinah). How God's presence was experienced in the Tenakh (the Tabernacle and the Temple) and how Jews understand and experience it today.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"The Shekhinah: God's presence on earth\n\"Shekhinah\" comes from the Hebrew root sh-kh-n, \"to dwell\" or \"to settle\". It means the presence of God dwelling among people. The word does not appear in the Tenakh but is used by the rabbis in the Talmud and Midrash. It describes how a God who is beyond the universe (transcendent) can also be close to people (immanent).\n\nIn the Tenakh\n• In the wilderness God told Moses, \"Let them make me a sanctuary, that I may dwell among them\" (Exodus 25:8). The word for dwell, v'shakhanti, shares the Shekhinah root.\n• When the Tabernacle (Mishkan) was finished, \"the cloud covered the Tent of Meeting, and the glory of the Lord filled the Tabernacle\" (Exodus 40:34). God's presence was also shown as a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night (Exodus 13:21).\n• When Solomon dedicated the First Temple in Jerusalem, a cloud filled the house and the priests could not stand to serve (1 Kings 8:10–11). The innermost room, the Holy of Holies, was thought of as the special place of God's presence. Only the High Priest entered it, once a year on Yom Kippur.\n• The prophets describe God's glory filling the Temple (Isaiah 6:1–3, \"the whole earth is full of his glory\").\n\nAfter the Temple\n• The Second Temple was destroyed by the Romans in 70 CE. The rabbis taught that the Shekhinah did not abandon Israel but went into exile with the people (Talmud, Megillah 29a).\n• The Shekhinah is found wherever Jews pray and study: \"If ten sit together and occupy themselves with Torah, the Shekhinah rests among them\", and even two (Pirkei Avot 3:2 and 3:6). This helps explain why a minyan (ten adults) is needed for communal prayer.\n• The Shekhinah is linked to acts of kindness. The Talmud teaches that God visits the sick, so Jews should do the same (Sotah 14a).\n• Some traditions link the Shekhinah with Shabbat. In Jewish mysticism (Kabbalah) the Shekhinah is the feminine aspect of God, and the Kabbalat Shabbat song Lecha Dodi welcomes the Sabbath \"bride\".\n\nDiversity of views\n• Orthodox Jews pray three times daily for the rebuilding of the Temple and the return of the Shekhinah to Zion. The Amidah includes the words \"who restores his Shekhinah to Zion\".\n• Many Reform and Liberal Jews do not pray for the Temple to be rebuilt. They understand the Shekhinah more as an experience of God's closeness in community, ethical action and nature.\n• Many Jews feel God's presence at the Western Wall (Kotel), the last remaining part of the Temple complex, where people place written prayers in the cracks.\n\nWhy it matters\nBelief in the Shekhinah reassures Jews that God is close and cares, even in suffering and exile. It gives meaning to communal worship, study and kindness, and it explains the importance of Jerusalem and the Temple Mount.\n\nKey terms: Shekhinah; Mishkan (Tabernacle); Holy of Holies; immanence; transcendence; minyan; Kotel."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","title":"Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","specFocus":"Key beliefs: Jewish beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection, and the different views held within Judaism (Orthodox and Reform).","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"Life after death in Judaism\nThe Tenakh says relatively little about life after death. Judaism focuses mainly on living well in this world by keeping the mitzvot. Even so, there is a long tradition of belief in a life beyond death, and Jews hold a range of views about it.\n\nKey terms\n• Sheol: in the early books of the Tenakh, a shadowy place of the dead where all people go (e.g. Psalm 6:5, Ecclesiastes 9:10).\n• Olam Ha-Ba: \"the world to come\". It can mean life after death, or the perfected world of the messianic age.\n• Gan Eden: \"the Garden of Eden\", a paradise where the souls of the righteous are with God.\n• Gehinnom: a place of purification, not eternal punishment. Rabbinic tradition says most souls spend no more than twelve months there (Mishnah Eduyot 2:10), which is why mourners say Kaddish for eleven months, so as not to suggest their parent needed the full twelve.\n• Techiyat ha-metim: \"resurrection of the dead\".\n\nResurrection\n• Daniel 12:2: \"Many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth will awake, some to everlasting life, others to reproach and everlasting contempt.\"\n• Ezekiel 37, the vision of the valley of dry bones coming back to life, is often read as a symbol of resurrection and of Israel's national renewal.\n• The Mishnah (Sanhedrin 10:1) says, \"All Israel have a share in the world to come\", but warns that someone who denies the resurrection of the dead loses that share.\n• Maimonides' 13th Principle of Faith: belief in the resurrection of the dead. The second blessing of the Amidah praises God \"who gives life to the dead\" (mechayeh ha-metim).\n• Many Orthodox Jews believe in a bodily resurrection when the Messiah comes. This is one reason they bury the body whole and do not allow cremation.\n\nJudgement\n• Jews believe God judges each person. At Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur God judges the living, writing people in the Book of Life.\n• After death the soul is judged on how the person lived: their keeping of the mitzvot, their kindness and their justice. \"The dust returns to the earth as it was, and the spirit returns to God who gave it\" (Ecclesiastes 12:7).\n• The rabbis taught that the righteous of all nations have a share in the world to come (Tosefta Sanhedrin 13:2). Non-Jews do not need to become Jewish to be rewarded, as long as they keep the seven Noahide laws.\n\nDiversity\n• Orthodox: affirm resurrection and judgement as principles of faith, and keep the traditional wording of the Amidah.\n• Reform and Liberal: many reject bodily resurrection and believe instead in the immortality of the soul, or that people live on through their deeds, children and memory. Some Reform prayer books offer \"who gives life to all\" (mechayeh hakol) in place of \"to the dead\". Reform Judaism permits cremation.\n• Many Jews of all groups say that it is impossible to know about the afterlife, and that what matters is this life. Pirkei Avot 4:17 says one hour of repentance and good deeds in this world is better than all the life of the world to come.\n\nInfluence\nThese beliefs encourage moral responsibility, shape burial and mourning (quick burial, no cremation for the Orthodox, Kaddish, yahrzeit) and offer comfort to the bereaved."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","title":"The nature and role of the Messiah","specFocus":"Key beliefs: the nature and role of the Messiah (Mashiach), including different views on the role and importance of the Messiah (Orthodox, Reform and other perspectives).","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"The Messiah (Mashiach)\nMashiach means \"anointed one\". In ancient Israel, kings and priests were anointed with oil to show that God had chosen them. Traditional Judaism teaches that God will send a future human leader, anointed as king and descended from King David, who will bring in a messianic age of peace.\n\nNature of the Messiah (traditional view)\n• A human being, not divine, and not a son of God in a literal sense.\n• A descendant of David: \"A shoot shall come out from the stump of Jesse\" (Isaiah 11:1). Jesse was David's father. See also Jeremiah 23:5.\n• A great leader who is wise, just and faithful to the Torah; \"the spirit of the Lord shall rest on him\" (Isaiah 11:2).\n\nRole of the Messiah\n• To gather the Jewish people from exile back to the land of Israel.\n• To rebuild the Temple in Jerusalem.\n• To bring universal peace: \"They shall beat their swords into ploughshares… nation shall not lift up sword against nation\" (Isaiah 2:4), and \"the wolf shall live with the lamb\" (Isaiah 11:6).\n• To lead the world to know and worship the one God: \"The earth will be full of the knowledge of the Lord\" (Isaiah 11:9).\n• The messianic age is often linked with the resurrection of the dead and Olam Ha-Ba.\n• Maimonides' 12th Principle: \"I believe with perfect faith in the coming of the Messiah, and though he may tarry, I will wait daily for his coming.\" In the Mishneh Torah he wrote that if a person fulfils these tasks, he is the Messiah; if he fails, he is not.\n\nWhy Jews do not accept Jesus as Messiah\nThe messianic tasks, such as world peace, the ingathering of exiles and the rebuilding of the Temple, have not been fulfilled. Jewish belief also does not accept a divine Messiah.\n\nDifferent views\n• Orthodox: expect a personal Messiah and pray daily for his coming (in the Amidah). Some, for example within the Chabad-Lubavitch movement, stress that his coming may be very near. Some Orthodox Jews believe that every generation has a person who could be the Messiah if the generation deserved it.\n• Religious Zionists: many see the founding of the State of Israel (1948) as \"the first flowering of our redemption\", a step towards the messianic age. The phrase comes from the Prayer for the State of Israel.\n• Some strictly Orthodox groups (e.g. Satmar, Neturei Karta) believe that a Jewish state should be set up only by the Messiah, and so they oppose modern Zionism on religious grounds.\n• Reform and Liberal: most reject a personal Messiah and speak instead of a messianic age that humanity must build through justice and tikkun olam. Some Reform prayer books replace \"redeemer\" (go'el) with \"redemption\" (ge'ulah).\n• Some Jews believe the messianic idea is a symbol of hope rather than a literal future event.\n\nInfluence today\nBelief in the Messiah gives hope in times of suffering. Many Jews sang Maimonides' words \"Ani Ma'amin\" (\"I believe\") in the Nazi camps. It motivates Jews to act justly to \"prepare the way\", and it is expressed in prayer, in hopes for Jerusalem and at Pesach, when Elijah, the traditional herald of the Messiah, is welcomed at the seder.\n\nKey terms: Mashiach; messianic age; ingathering of exiles; Ani Ma'amin; Zionism; tikkun olam."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","title":"The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham","specFocus":"The Covenant and the mitzvot: the promised land and the Covenant with Abraham, Genesis 12:1–3 (prescribed text), and its importance for Jews today.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"What is a covenant?\nA covenant (brit) is a binding agreement between God and people, with promises and responsibilities on both sides. Judaism understands the relationship between God and the Jewish people as a covenant relationship.\n\nAbraham\nAbraham (first called Abram) lived about 4,000 years ago in Mesopotamia (Ur, then Haran). Jewish tradition says he came to recognise that there is only one God and rejected the idols of his father Terah. He is called Avraham Avinu, \"Abraham our father\", the first of the patriarchs (Abraham, Isaac and Jacob).\n\nThe prescribed text: Genesis 12:1–3\n\"The Lord said to Abram: Go forth from your land, from your birthplace and from your father's house, to the land that I will show you. I will make of you a great nation, and I will bless you; I will make your name great, and you shall be a blessing. I will bless those who bless you, and curse him that curses you; and all the families of the earth shall be blessed through you.\"\nGod's promises:\n1. Land: \"the land that I will show you\", later identified as Canaan (Genesis 12:7; 15:18).\n2. Nation: Abram's descendants will become \"a great nation\".\n3. Blessing: Abraham will be blessed and will be a blessing, and all the families of the earth will be blessed through him.\nAbraham's part: faith and obedience. He leaves everything and goes, trusting God.\n\nThe covenant develops\n• Genesis 15: the \"covenant between the pieces\". God promises descendants as many as the stars and the land \"from the river of Egypt to the great river, the Euphrates\".\n• Genesis 17: God changes Abram's name to Abraham (\"father of many nations\") and gives the sign of the covenant: circumcision of every male at eight days old (brit milah), \"an everlasting covenant\". God promises \"all the land of Canaan, for an everlasting possession\" (17:8).\n• Genesis 22 (the binding of Isaac, the Akedah) is the supreme test of Abraham's faith. It is read on Rosh Hashanah.\n\nThe promised land\n• Eretz Yisrael, the land of Israel, is central to Jewish identity. Jews pray facing Jerusalem, and many end the Pesach seder and Yom Kippur with \"Next year in Jerusalem\".\n• After centuries of exile, the modern State of Israel was founded in 1948. Its Law of Return (1950) allows any Jew to settle there.\n• Views differ. Many Jews see Israel as the fulfilment of God's promise; others, including many secular Zionists, see it in historical and political terms. Some strictly Orthodox groups believe a Jewish state should wait for the Messiah. Many Reform Jews value Israel deeply while stressing the universal side of the covenant (\"all families of the earth shall be blessed\").\n• Many Jews disagree about Israeli government policies; supporting the land is not the same as supporting every political decision.\n\nImportance today\n• Brit milah continues the sign of the covenant for Jewish boys today.\n• Jews see themselves as the descendants of Abraham, with a responsibility to be \"a blessing\" through ethical living.\n• Abraham is a model of faith, of hospitality (Genesis 18, welcoming three strangers) and of justice (arguing for Sodom, Genesis 18:25).\n\nKey terms: brit (covenant); Avraham Avinu; patriarchs; Eretz Yisrael; brit milah; Akedah; Zionism."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","title":"The Covenant at Sinai, the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments","specFocus":"The Covenant and the mitzvot: the Covenant at Sinai and its importance, including the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments, Exodus 20:1–17 (prescribed text).","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"Background\nThe Israelites, descendants of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, had become slaves in Egypt. God called Moses at the burning bush (Exodus 3) to lead them out. After the ten plagues and the crossing of the Sea of Reeds, they reached Mount Sinai about three months later (Exodus 19).\n\nThe Covenant at Sinai\n• God offered the covenant: \"If you will obey my voice and keep my covenant, you shall be my treasured possession among all peoples… a kingdom of priests and a holy nation\" (Exodus 19:5–6).\n• The people accepted: \"All that the Lord has spoken we will do, and we will understand\" (na'aseh v'nishma, Exodus 24:7). Jews see this as a commitment to obey even before understanding everything.\n• God gave the Ten Commandments (Aseret ha-Dibrot, \"the ten statements\") amid thunder, fire and the sound of the shofar (Exodus 19:16–19). Moses received the rest of the Torah on the mountain over forty days. The two stone tablets were placed in the Ark of the Covenant.\n• Unlike the covenant with Abraham, which was made with one family, the Sinai covenant was made with the whole nation, and it turned the Israelites into a people defined by God's law.\n• Jewish tradition teaches that the souls of all future Jews were present at Sinai (based on Deuteronomy 29:13–14), so every Jew is bound by the covenant.\n\nThe role of Moses\n• He was the leader who brought the people out of Egypt, the lawgiver, and the greatest prophet: \"There has not arisen a prophet since in Israel like Moses, whom the Lord knew face to face\" (Deuteronomy 34:10). Maimonides' 7th Principle affirms this.\n• He was the mediator between God and the people, and he pleaded for them after the golden calf (Exodus 32).\n• Jews call him Moshe Rabbenu, \"Moses our teacher\". He is honoured but never worshipped.\n• Orthodox Jews believe Moses received both the written Torah and the oral Torah (later written down in the Mishnah and Talmud). Reform Jews generally see the Torah as having developed over time, with Moses as its inspiring founder.\n\nThe Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:1–17), in the Jewish numbering\n1. \"I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of slavery.\"\n2. You shall have no other gods before me, and you shall not make idols.\n3. Do not take the name of the Lord your God in vain.\n4. Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy.\n5. Honour your father and your mother.\n6. Do not murder.\n7. Do not commit adultery.\n8. Do not steal (the rabbis understood this as kidnapping).\n9. Do not bear false witness against your neighbour.\n10. Do not covet.\nThe first tablet is usually understood to hold the duties towards God (1–5, with honouring parents seen as linked to honouring God), and the second the duties towards other people (6–10).\n\nImportance today\n• The Ten Commandments are part of the 613 mitzvot and are seen as headings for the whole Torah.\n• They are read in synagogue on Shavuot, the festival that celebrates the giving of the Torah at Sinai, and when the passages come round in the annual Torah cycle; the congregation stands.\n• Their moral principles (no murder, theft or lying) underpin Jewish ethics and have influenced the laws of many countries.\n• Many synagogues display two tablets above the Aron Hakodesh."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","title":"Key moral principles: justice, healing the world, charity and kindness to others","specFocus":"The Covenant and the mitzvot: key moral principles including justice (tzedek), healing the world (tikkun olam), charity (tzedakah) and kindness to others (gemilut chasadim), and how Jews put them into practice today.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"Ethics at the heart of the covenant\nJews believe that keeping the covenant means more than ritual: God requires moral behaviour. \"He has told you, O mortal, what is good, and what does the Lord require of you but to do justice, to love kindness and to walk humbly with your God\" (Micah 6:8).\n\nJustice (tzedek)\n• \"Justice, justice you shall pursue\" (Deuteronomy 16:20). The word is repeated for emphasis, and the rabbis said it means pursuing justice by just means.\n• Justice means fairness, honesty and protecting the vulnerable: the widow, the orphan and the stranger (\"you were strangers in the land of Egypt\", Exodus 22:21 and Deuteronomy 10:19). Courts must not favour rich or poor (Leviticus 19:15).\n• Justice is part of God's own nature (God as Judge), so Jews imitate God by acting justly.\n• Today: Jewish involvement in human rights, anti-racism and refugee campaigns (for example the Jewish Council for Racial Equality and René Cassin in the UK).\n\nHealing the world (tikkun olam)\n• Tikkun olam means \"repairing\" or \"healing\" the world. The phrase appears in the Mishnah, where it describes laws made \"for the betterment of the world\" (Gittin 4:2). The Aleinu prayer speaks of perfecting the world under the kingship of God (l'takken olam). In 16th-century Kabbalah (Isaac Luria) it describes humans gathering the \"sparks\" of holiness scattered at creation.\n• Today it means working with God as partners to make the world better: social action, care for the environment and peace-building. It is especially emphasised by Reform and Liberal Judaism, but it is shared widely.\n• Pirkei Avot 2:16: \"It is not your duty to finish the work, but neither are you free to desist from it.\"\n• Example: Mitzvah Day, a UK-founded day of community volunteering in which thousands of Jews and people of other faiths take part each November.\n\nCharity (tzedakah)\n• Tzedakah comes from the same root as tzedek (justice). Giving is not optional generosity but a duty of justice, because wealth ultimately belongs to God.\n• \"Do not harden your heart or shut your hand against your poor brother… open your hand\" (Deuteronomy 15:7–8). Farmers had to leave the corners of fields and the gleanings for the poor (Leviticus 19:9–10).\n• Many Jews give about a tenth (ma'aser) of their income. Homes often have a tzedakah box (pushke), and coins are added before Shabbat.\n• Maimonides' Eight Levels of Giving: the highest is helping someone become self-sufficient (a loan, partnership or job); next is giving where neither giver nor receiver knows the other; the lowest is giving unwillingly.\n• Examples: Jewish Care, World Jewish Relief, food banks run by synagogues.\n\nKindness to others (gemilut chasadim)\n• Gemilut chasadim means acts of loving-kindness, done personally and without expecting a reward: visiting the sick (bikur cholim), comforting mourners, hospitality (hachnasat orchim, modelled on Abraham in Genesis 18), helping at weddings and funerals.\n• \"Love your neighbour as yourself\" (Leviticus 19:18). Hillel: \"What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour; that is the whole Torah, the rest is commentary – go and learn\" (Shabbat 31a).\n• Pirkei Avot 1:2: the world stands on three things: Torah, worship (avodah) and acts of loving-kindness.\n• The rabbis said gemilut chasadim is greater than tzedakah, because it can be done for rich and poor, living and dead, and with one's body as well as one's money (Sukkah 49b).\n\nDiversity\nOrthodox and Reform Jews share these principles. Reform Judaism often presents ethical mitzvot as the heart of Judaism, while Orthodox Judaism sees ethics and ritual as equally commanded and bound together."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","title":"The sanctity of human life, including 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh)","specFocus":"The Covenant and the mitzvot: the importance of the sanctity of human life, including the concept of 'saving a life' (Pikuach Nefesh), and how it applies to issues such as Shabbat, medicine and organ donation.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"Sanctity of life\nThe sanctity of life is the belief that human life is holy and precious because it is given by God.\n• Humans are created \"in the image of God\" (Genesis 1:27), and God breathed \"the breath of life\" into the first human (Genesis 2:7).\n• \"Whoever sheds the blood of a human, by a human shall his blood be shed, for in the image of God he made humanity\" (Genesis 9:6).\n• The sixth commandment: \"Do not murder\" (Exodus 20:13).\n• Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5: Adam was created alone to teach that \"whoever destroys a single life is considered as if he destroyed an entire world, and whoever saves a single life is considered as if he saved an entire world.\" Each person is unique and of infinite value.\n• \"The Lord gave and the Lord has taken away; blessed be the name of the Lord\" (Job 1:21). Only God should decide when life ends.\n\nPikuach nefesh (\"saving a life\")\n• Pikuach nefesh is the principle that saving a human life overrides almost every other commandment.\n• Source: \"You shall keep my laws and my rules, by which a person shall live\" (Leviticus 18:5). The rabbis added \"and not die by them\" (Talmud Yoma 85b). The mitzvot are meant to give life, not end it.\n• \"Do not stand idly by the blood of your neighbour\" (Leviticus 19:16) means there is a duty to act when someone is in danger.\n• Examples: breaking Shabbat to call an ambulance or drive someone to hospital; eating on Yom Kippur if fasting would endanger health (for example a pregnant woman or someone with diabetes, after medical advice); taking non-kosher medicine; postponing brit milah if a baby is unwell.\n• The rabbis taught that the duty applies even when it is not certain a life is at risk, and that the most observant people should act first so no one hesitates.\n• Exceptions: a Jew must not commit murder, idolatry or forbidden sexual relations (such as adultery or incest) even to save their own life (Talmud Sanhedrin 74a).\n\nApplications today\n• Medicine: Judaism strongly supports medical treatment and doctors. Hatzola volunteer ambulance services operate in Jewish areas of London and Manchester.\n• Organ donation: most authorities allow or encourage donation from a living donor, and donation after death when it saves a life. Orthodox rabbis debate how death is defined (brain-stem death or the heart stopping). Under the UK \"opt-out\" system (in England since 2020), people can record that their faith should be taken into account, and Jewish organisations have produced guidance for families.\n• Euthanasia: active euthanasia is generally forbidden because it ends life deliberately. Many authorities allow treatment that only prolongs dying to be withheld, and they allow strong pain relief. Some Reform and Liberal Jews take a more permissive view of the right to choose.\n• Abortion: most Jews believe that a fetus is not yet a full person until birth and that the mother's life takes priority (Mishnah Oholot 7:6). Orthodox Judaism allows abortion where the mother's life or health is at serious risk. Reform Judaism allows a wider range of reasons, leaving it to the woman's conscience.\n\nKey terms: sanctity of life; b'tselem Elohim; pikuach nefesh; Hatzola."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","title":"The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","specFocus":"The Covenant and the mitzvot: the relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot – how humans choose whether to obey God's commandments, and Orthodox and Reform views on how binding the mitzvot are.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"The 613 mitzvot\n• A mitzvah (plural mitzvot) is a commandment. The Talmud (Makkot 23b) teaches that the Torah contains 613 mitzvot: 248 positive commands (\"you shall…\"), linked to the number of parts of the body, and 365 negative commands (\"you shall not…\"), linked to the days of the year. So the mitzvot are meant to involve the whole person all year round.\n• Maimonides listed them in his Sefer ha-Mitzvot. Many cannot be kept today, for example the Temple sacrifices, and some apply only to priests, to men or women, or to the land of Israel.\n• The mitzvot cover worship (Shabbat, prayer, festivals), food (kashrut), family life and ethics (charity, honesty, justice).\n• \"Mitzvah\" is also used loosely to mean a good deed.\n\nFree will (bechirah)\n• Jews believe God created humans with the freedom to choose between good and evil: \"I have set before you life and death, blessing and curse; therefore choose life\" (Deuteronomy 30:19).\n• To Cain God said, \"Sin crouches at the door… but you can master it\" (Genesis 4:7), so humans are responsible for their choices.\n• Rabbi Akiva: \"Everything is foreseen, yet freedom of choice is given\" (Pirkei Avot 3:15). God knows everything, but humans still choose.\n• Maimonides wrote that every person can become as righteous as Moses or as wicked as Jeroboam; no one is forced (Laws of Repentance 5:2).\n• The rabbis taught that humans have two inclinations: the yetzer ha-tov (good inclination) and the yetzer ha-ra (evil or selfish inclination). The yetzer ha-ra is not wholly bad: without it, said the Midrash, no one would build a house, marry or have children (Genesis Rabbah 9:7). The Torah and mitzvot help people direct it well.\n• \"Everything is in the hands of heaven except the fear of heaven\" (Talmud Berakhot 33b): God controls much of life, but whether we obey God is our own choice.\n\nHow free will relates to the mitzvot\n• Commandments only make sense if people are free to obey or disobey. Without free will, reward, punishment and repentance would be meaningless.\n• Keeping the mitzvot is the way to use free will well: it trains the yetzer ha-tov and gives life holiness.\n• God judges people on their choices (Rosh Hashanah, Yom Kippur), and teshuvah (repentance) is always possible.\n• At Sinai the people freely accepted the covenant: \"we will do and we will understand\" (Exodus 24:7).\n\nDiversity\n• Orthodox: all 613 mitzvot (as many as can be kept today) are commanded by God and binding. Humans are free to choose whether to obey, but they are not free to decide which commands are valid. Halakhah (Jewish law), developed by the rabbis, guides how to keep them.\n• Reform: the mitzvot are a divinely inspired guide. Individuals use their free will and conscience to make \"informed choices\" about which ritual mitzvot to keep, while the ethical mitzvot remain binding. Reform Judaism has dropped some laws, for example on the separation of men and women in worship.\n• Some Jews keep mitzvot for cultural or family reasons rather than as commandments.\n\nKey terms: mitzvah; 613 mitzvot; halakhah; bechirah (free will); yetzer ha-tov; yetzer ha-ra; teshuvah."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper1-1-10","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","title":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","specFocus":"The Covenant and the mitzvot: mitzvot between man and God (bein adam la-Makom) and mitzvot between man and man (bein adam la-chavero), including the difference between them and their importance.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"Two kinds of mitzvot\nThe rabbis divided the mitzvot into two broad groups:\n• Mitzvot bein adam la-Makom: between a person and God (\"Makom\", \"the Place\", is a rabbinic name for God). These concern worship and holiness: belief in one God, not making idols, not misusing God's name, prayer, keeping Shabbat and festivals, kashrut, wearing tefillin and tzitzit, fixing a mezuzah, and studying Torah.\n• Mitzvot bein adam la-chavero: between a person and their fellow human. These concern how people treat each other: honouring parents, not murdering, stealing, lying or gossiping (lashon ha-ra), giving tzedakah, visiting the sick, paying workers on time, honest weights and measures, and loving one's neighbour (Leviticus 19:18).\n\nThe Ten Commandments\nTraditionally the two tablets reflect this division. The first five (from \"I am the Lord your God\" to \"Honour your father and your mother\") concern God; honouring parents is placed here because parents are partners with God in creating a child. The second five (\"Do not murder\"… \"Do not covet\") concern other people.\n\nThe difference between them\n• Atonement: the Mishnah teaches, \"For sins between a person and God, Yom Kippur atones; for sins between a person and their fellow, Yom Kippur does not atone until they have made peace with their fellow\" (Mishnah Yoma 8:9). So before Yom Kippur Jews apologise to anyone they have wronged and try to put things right, for example by returning anything stolen.\n• Focus: one group is directed at God (holiness, worship), the other at society (justice, kindness).\n• Some mitzvot between people may be set aside less easily. For example, the rabbis taught that human dignity (kavod ha-briyot) can override some rabbinic rules.\n\nLinks between them\n• Both come from the same God, are part of the same Torah and are equally commanded.\n• Treating people well is itself a way of serving God, because every person is made in God's image (Genesis 1:27). The rabbis taught that whoever insults a person insults their Creator (Proverbs 17:5, \"whoever mocks the poor insults his Maker\").\n• Love of God and love of neighbour are linked: Deuteronomy 6:5 (\"love the Lord your God\") and Leviticus 19:18 (\"love your neighbour as yourself\"). Leviticus 19 itself mixes both kinds, holiness laws and ethical laws side by side, under the heading \"Be holy, for I the Lord your God am holy\" (19:2).\n• The prophets warned that ritual worship without justice is worthless (Isaiah 1:11–17; Amos 5:21–24).\n• Hillel summed up the Torah as \"What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour\" (Shabbat 31a), and Rabbi Akiva called love of neighbour \"a great principle of the Torah\".\n\nImportance and diversity\n• Orthodox Jews see both kinds as equally binding, and ritual and ethical laws are woven together in halakhah.\n• Reform Jews tend to place greater emphasis on the ethical mitzvot (between people) as the lasting core of the covenant, while seeing ritual mitzvot as valuable ways to strengthen the relationship with God, subject to informed choice.\n• Most Jews would say that a person who is ritually observant but dishonest or unkind has not truly kept the covenant.\n\nKey terms: bein adam la-Makom; bein adam la-chavero; lashon ha-ra; Mishnah Yoma 8:9; kavod ha-briyot (human dignity)."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","title":"The synagogue and its importance","specFocus":"The synagogue and worship: the synagogue and its importance for Jewish individuals and communities, as a house of prayer, study and assembly.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"What is a synagogue?\n\"Synagogue\" is a Greek word meaning \"bringing together\". Jews often call it shul (Yiddish, \"school\"), or in Reform and Liberal communities a temple. Synagogues probably began during the Babylonian exile (6th century BCE) and became central after the destruction of the Second Temple in 70 CE, when prayer replaced sacrifice.\n\nThree Hebrew names, three purposes\n1. Beit Tefilah, house of prayer: communal services, especially on Shabbat and festivals; daily services where there is a minyan. Prayer is called \"the service of the heart\" (Talmud Ta'anit 2a), the replacement for Temple sacrifice: \"we will offer the words of our lips instead of bulls\" (Hosea 14:3).\n2. Beit Midrash, house of study: classes, Torah study, cheder (religion school) for children, and preparation for Bar and Bat Mitzvah. Study of Torah is itself a form of worship.\n3. Beit Knesset, house of assembly: the community centre for social events, youth clubs, kiddush after services, charity collections, support for the elderly and bereaved, celebrations of life-cycle events, and sometimes kosher catering.\n\nWhy the synagogue matters\n• It holds the Torah scrolls in the Aron Hakodesh (ark), the holiest objects in Judaism.\n• It is called a mikdash me'at, a \"small sanctuary\" (based on Ezekiel 11:16), echoing the Temple. Its holiness comes from prayer and Torah, not from the building itself.\n• Communal prayer is valued highly. Certain prayers, such as the Kaddish, the Kedushah and the reading of the Torah, need a minyan of ten adults. Orthodox synagogues count ten men; Reform, Liberal and most Masorti synagogues count women and men equally.\n• The rabbis taught that the Shekhinah rests among ten who pray together (Pirkei Avot 3:6; Berakhot 6a).\n• It strengthens Jewish identity in a minority community, which is particularly important in Britain, where Jews are a small minority (about 270,000 in England and Wales in the 2021 census).\n• The rabbi is a teacher and pastor, not a priest. A service can be led by any qualified member, and the chazan (cantor) leads singing in some synagogues.\n\nDiversity in Britain\n• Orthodox umbrella bodies include the United Synagogue (led by the Chief Rabbi). Strictly Orthodox (Haredi) communities have many small shtiebels, especially in Stamford Hill and Manchester. Sephardi communities include Bevis Marks (London, 1701), the oldest synagogue in continuous use in the UK.\n• Progressive movements: Reform Judaism and Liberal Judaism, which have been coming together under the name Progressive Judaism. Masorti Judaism is a traditional but non-Orthodox movement.\n• Orthodox Jews walk to synagogue on Shabbat, so Orthodox communities usually live within walking distance of one. Reform members may drive.\n\nDoes a Jew need a synagogue?\nMuch of Judaism is home-centred (Shabbat meals, the Pesach seder, kashrut), and private prayer is valid anywhere. However, the synagogue supports communal prayer, learning and support, which many see as vital for Jewish continuity.\n\nKey terms: Beit Tefilah; Beit Midrash; Beit Knesset; shul; minyan; mikdash me'at; rabbi; chazan."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-judaism-paper2-2-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","title":"The design and religious features of synagogues","specFocus":"The synagogue and worship: the design and religious features of synagogues, including the bimah (reading platform), aron hakodesh (ark) and ner tamid (ever-burning light), and associated practices; differences between Orthodox and Reform synagogues.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"Design principles\nSynagogues vary greatly in architecture, but they share key features. There are no statues or pictures of God or people, because of the commandment against images (Exodus 20:4). Decoration uses Hebrew lettering, patterns, the Star of David, the menorah, the lions of Judah and plants. Most synagogues are built so that worshippers face Jerusalem (in Britain, roughly south-east), following Solomon's prayer (1 Kings 8:44) and the Talmud (Berakhot 30a).\n\nKey features\n• Aron Hakodesh (holy ark): a cupboard on the wall facing Jerusalem that holds the Sefer Torah scrolls. It recalls the Ark of the Covenant, which held the tablets of the Ten Commandments in the Temple. It is the focal point of the synagogue. It is covered by a decorated curtain, the parochet (based on the curtain in the Tabernacle, Exodus 26:31–33). The congregation stands when the ark is opened, and opening it is an honour.\n• Sefer Torah: a handwritten parchment scroll of the five books of Moses, written by a trained scribe (sofer). It is dressed in a mantle (Ashkenazi) or kept in a case (Sephardi), with a breastplate and crowns or finials (rimonim). It is not touched directly; a yad (pointer) is used to follow the words. By custom, if a scroll is dropped, those present may fast. Worn-out scrolls are buried.\n• Ner tamid (ever-burning light): a light, usually above the ark, that is never put out. It recalls the menorah in the Temple, which was to burn \"continually\" (Exodus 27:20–21), and symbolises God's eternal presence (Shekhinah) and the light of Torah.\n• Bimah (reading platform): a raised platform with a reading desk from which the Torah is read and services are often led. Raising it shows respect for the Torah and helps everyone hear (Nehemiah 8:4, Ezra stood on a wooden platform).\n• Other features: a display of the Ten Commandments (two tablets) above the ark; a menorah (seven-branched lamp, echoing the Temple); seats facing the ark or the bimah; memorial boards; a pulpit.\n\nAssociated practices\n• The Torah is carried around the synagogue (hakafah) before and after reading, and people touch it with their tallit or siddur and kiss it.\n• Hagbah: lifting the open scroll to show the congregation, who say \"This is the Torah which Moses set before the children of Israel.\"\n• Seven people are called up for an aliyah on Shabbat morning to say blessings over the reading.\n\nOrthodox and Reform differences\n• Bimah: in Orthodox synagogues it is traditionally in the centre, showing the Torah at the heart of the community, and the reader faces the ark. In most Reform synagogues the bimah and ark are together at the front, often like a stage, with the leader facing the congregation.\n• Seating: Orthodox synagogues separate men and women with a mechitzah (screen) or a women's gallery, so worshippers can concentrate on prayer. Reform and Liberal synagogues have mixed seating; families sit together.\n• Music: Orthodox synagogues sing without instruments on Shabbat (as a sign of mourning for the Temple and because playing instruments is avoided on Shabbat). Many Reform synagogues have an organ or other instruments and a mixed choir.\n• Leadership: in Reform synagogues women may be rabbis or cantors and read from the Torah at the bimah. Orthodox synagogues have male rabbis, although some now have female educators or advisors.\n• Name: some Reform communities call the building a \"temple\".\n\nKey terms: Aron Hakodesh; parochet; Sefer Torah; sofer; yad; ner tamid; bimah; mechitzah; aliyah; hagbah."}],"rubrics":{"Beliefs":{"1":"1 mark (AO1), multiple choice: 1 mark for the correct option. No credit if more than one option is chosen.","12":"12 marks (AO2), 'Evaluate this statement': marked by levels, with up to 3 further marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar. Level 4 (10–12): a well-argued answer that considers different points of view in reasoned detail, uses logical chains of reasoning and reaches a judgement supported by accurate knowledge of religious teaching. Level 3 (7–9): reasoned consideration of different points of view, with logical chains of reasoning drawing on knowledge of religious teaching. Level 2 (4–6): reasoned consideration of one point of view, or some recognition of different points of view, each backed by relevant reasons or evidence. Level 1 (1–3): a point of view supported by one or more simple reasons. A 'Jewish' perspective must be included; a justified conclusion is expected for the top level.","2":"2 marks (AO1), 'Give two…': 1 mark for each correct, relevant point, up to 2. No development needed. Repeated points or points that are not distinct gain no extra credit.","4":"4 marks (AO1), 'Explain two ways…' or 'Explain two contrasting…': for each of two ways or beliefs, 1 mark for a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate point, or 2 marks for a detailed explanation. Where contrasting beliefs are asked for, the two must differ (for example Orthodox and Reform).","5":"5 marks (AO1), 'Explain two… Refer to sacred writings…': for each of two beliefs or teachings, 1 mark for a simple explanation or 2 marks for a detailed explanation, plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching (for example Torah, Tenakh, Talmud, Mishnah, siddur or a respected rabbi). The reference may be paraphrased."},"Practices":{"1":"1 mark (AO1), multiple choice: 1 mark for the correct option. No credit if more than one option is chosen.","12":"12 marks (AO2), 'Evaluate this statement': marked by levels, with up to 3 further marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar. Level 4 (10–12): a well-argued answer that considers different points of view in reasoned detail, uses logical chains of reasoning and reaches a judgement supported by accurate knowledge of religious teaching and practice. Level 3 (7–9): reasoned consideration of different points of view, with logical chains of reasoning drawing on knowledge of religious teaching. Level 2 (4–6): reasoned consideration of one point of view, or some recognition of different points of view, each backed by relevant reasons or evidence. Level 1 (1–3): a point of view supported by one or more simple reasons. A 'Jewish' perspective must be included; a justified conclusion is expected for the top level.","2":"2 marks (AO1), 'Give two…': 1 mark for each correct, relevant point, up to 2. No development needed. Repeated points or points that are not distinct gain no extra credit.","4":"4 marks (AO1), 'Explain two ways…' or 'Explain two contrasting…': for each of two ways or practices, 1 mark for a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate point, or 2 marks for a detailed explanation. Where contrasting practices are asked for, the two must differ (for example Orthodox and Reform synagogues).","5":"5 marks (AO1), 'Explain two… Refer to sacred writings…': for each of two points, 1 mark for a simple explanation or 2 marks for a detailed explanation, plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching (for example Torah, Tenakh, Talmud, Mishnah, siddur or Haggadah). The reference may be paraphrased."}},"markingSchema":{"dsl":"SCHEME AQA Religious Studies adaptable guidance\nCOMMAND ALL\nMARKS AUTO\nREQUIRE use accurate subject knowledge and answer the command word\nREQUIRE support religious or non-religious views where the question asks for them\nNEVER invent quotations, sources or official level descriptors\nFEEDBACK be specific, supportive and honest about missing content\n\nCOMMAND Explain\nMARKS AUTO\nAWARD 1 one accurate relevant point\nAWARD 1 a clear linked explanation\n\nCOMMAND Evaluate\nMARKS AUTO\nBAND 9-12 well-argued evaluation with a justified conclusion\nBAND 5-8 relevant discussion with some evaluation\nBAND 1-4 limited points with little evaluation","id":"aqa-rs-generic","references":[{"title":"AQA Religious Studies 8062 specification","url":"https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/religious-studies/gcse/religious-studies-8062/specification"},{"title":"AQA scheme of assessment","url":"https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/religious-studies/gcse/religious-studies-8062/specification/scheme-of-assessment"}],"sourceNotes":"AQA GCSE RS A uses 1/1/4/6/12 question structures and a separate SPaG overlay. Check the current official specification and mark scheme before relying on a mark estimate.","status":"adaptable","title":"AQA Religious Studies · check and adapt","version":1},"moduleCounts":{"paper1":10,"paper2":2},"meta":{"modulesTotal":12,"paper1QuestionCount":100,"paper2QuestionCount":20,"provenance":"Original practice questions written to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification (section 3.1.6 Judaism, and the Component 1 question structure of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks). They are not taken from AQA past papers, specimen papers, mark schemes, textbooks or revision guides. Model answers and revision notes are original indicative exemplars, not official AQA mark schemes; the rubric text paraphrases the published assessment structure. Short quotations from the Tenakh, Mishnah, Talmud and siddur are cited in the evidence lists.","qualification":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062), Component 1: The study of religions – Judaism (beliefs and teachings; practices)","questionTotal":120,"title":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A – Judaism"}});
